const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "D", "2": "E", "3": "F", "4": "E", "5": "E", "6": "B", "7": "A", "8": "A", "9": "E", "10": "E", "11": "B", "12": "A", "13": "C", "14": "E", "15": "A", "16": "B", "17": "C", "18": "F", "19": "B", "20": "B", "21": "C", "22": "A", "23": "E", "24": "A", "25": "C", "26": "E", "27": "C", "28": "E", "29": "E", "30": "D", "31": "E", "32": "D", "33": "A", "34": "A", "35": "D", "36": "D", "37": "C", "38": "C", "39": "C", "40": "E", "41": "D", "42": "E", "43": "H", "44": "A", "45": "A", "46": "E", "47": "D", "48": "C", "49": "C", "50": "B"};
const EXPLANATIONS = {"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute hemolytic transfusion reactions are the result of the transfusion of ABO incompatible erythrocytes. When this occurs, preformed antibodies against donor antigens on the surface of incompatible erythrocytes lead to profound hemolysis and a variety of symptoms including dyspnea, flank pain, renal failure, DIC, and potentially death. Treatment involves immediate cessation of the transfusion and supportive care.</p><p>Preformed antibodies to transfused red blood cells are the most likely cause of this patient's acute onset shortness of breath and back pain, which is most concerning for an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction (AHTR). AHTR is a life-threatening condition that occurs because of ABO incompatibility, which is often the result of clerical error. Patients with type A blood possess preformed antibodies against the B antigen on the surface of erythrocytes, while patients with type B blood possess antibodies against the A antigen. Those with AB blood lack preformed antibodies to both antigens, as they possess them on their own erythrocytes. Patients with type O blood have antibodies against both A and B antigen. Given this, it is critical that patients receive the appropriate type of blood for their specific blood type. As an example, patients with type A blood can only receive type A or type O blood from a donor, but not type B or AB blood. ABO incompatibility leads to AHTR, which results in profound hemolysis and often presents with flank pain, fever, hemoglobinuria, and a positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test, but may rapidly progress to disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC), shock, and death. Treatment includes immediate cessation of the transfusion followed by supportive care. The blood bank should be contacted immediately.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, and C.</p><p>Endotoxin release from infused bacteria (Choice A) is more likely to present with rigors and fevers rather than flank pain and dyspnea. Additionally, transfusion-related sepsis is much more common in patients receiving platelets rather than packed red blood cells.</p><p>Immune response to transfused plasma proteins (Choice B) may refer to either an anaphylactic reaction to donor immunoglobulins, as might occur in patients with IgA deficiency, or an inflammatory cascade from the presence of inflammatory cytokines in donor plasma that occurs in febrile nonhemolytic transfusion reactions. In either case, this patient is more likely to be suffering from AHTR.</p><p>Intra-abdominal hemorrhage (Choice C) is unlikely in this patient with a benign abdominal examination and normal blood pressure.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: TRALI is a syndrome characterized by neutrophil priming and activation within the lung parenchyma following exposure to inflammatory components in donor blood products, which leads to diffuse endothelial dysfunction and non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Patients often present with acute onset dyspnea, hypoxemia, fever, and cyanosis within minutes to hours after starting transfusion. Treatment is supportive.</p><p>Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) is the most likely diagnosis in this patient with sudden onset dyspnea, hypoxia, and fever following transfusion of packed red blood cells and plasma. TRALI is a syndrome characterized by respiratory distress, often with associated hypoxemia, cyanosis, fever, and new infiltrates on chest x-ray, that develops within 6 hours of transfusion of any blood product, including erythrocytes, fresh frozen plasma, or platelets. Frequently, symptoms develop within minutes to hours after starting transfusion. The pathogenesis of TRALI is thought to involve abnormal priming of neutrophils in the lung, which, when exposed to inflammatory cytokines present in transfused blood products, undergo widespread activation within the lung parenchyma. Release of cytokines and reactive oxygen species among other inflammatory components from activated neutrophils causes endothelial dysfunction and rapidly leads to non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. This contrasts with the pulmonary edema that develops in patients who develop respiratory failure secondary to transfusion associated cardiac overload (TACO). Treatment is with supportive care, which may include implementing non-invasive or invasive ventilation, vasopressors, and antipyretics. Importantly, patients who recover from TRALI can receive additional blood products, but these products should not be from the same prior blood donor.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction (Choice A) results from the transfusion of ABO incompatible erythrocytes. When this occurs, preformed antibodies against donor antigens on the surface of incompatible erythrocytes leads to profound hemolysis and a variety of symptoms including dyspnea, flank pain, dark urine, disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC), and possible death. This patient does not demonstrate any evidence of hemolysis.</p><p>re) 1s a syndrome characterized by respiratory distress, often with associated hypoxemia, cyanosis, fever, and new infiltrates on chest x-ray, that develops within 6 hours of transfusion of any blood product, including erythrocytes, fresh frozen plasma, or platelets. Frequently, symptoms develop within minutes to hours after starting transfusion. The pathogenesis of TRALI is thought to involve abnormal priming of neutrophils in the lung, which, when exposed to inflammatory cytokines present in transfused blood products, undergo widespread activation within the lung parenchyma. Release of cytokines and reactive oxygen species among other inflammatory components from activated neutrophils causes endothelial dysfunction and rapidly leads to non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. This contrasts with the pulmonary edema that develops in patients who develop respiratory failure secondary to transfusion associated cardiac overload (TACO). Treatment is with supportive care, which may include implementing non-invasive or invasive ventilation, vasopressors, and antipyretics. Importantly, patients who recover from TRALI can receive additional blood products, but these products should not be from the same prior blood donor.</p><p>Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction (Choice A) results from the transfusion of ABO incompatible erythrocytes. When this occurs, preformed antibodies against donor antigens on the surface of incompatible erythrocytes leads to profound hemolysis and a variety of symptoms including dyspnea, flank pain, dark urine, disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC), and possible death. This patient does not demonstrate any evidence of hemolysis.</p><p>Pneumonia (Choice B) is diagnosed in patients with fevers, cough, dyspnea, and a new focal consolidation on chest imaging. Symptoms usually develop insidiously over the course of several days. This patient's acute dyspnea, cyanosis, and hypoxemia during blood transfusion is more suggestive of TRALI.</p><p>Pulmonary embolism (PE) (Choice C) refers to a blood clot that has embolized from the venous system to one or more of the pulmonary arteries. The severity of presentation depends largely on the size and location of the thrombus, but patients may present with tachycardia, hypoxemia, and chest pain. Diffuse crackles on pulmonary examination would be unusual for a PE. Additionally, the temporal association of her pulmonary symptoms with transfusion is more suggestive of TRALI.</p><p>Sepsis (Choice D) is a syndrome of organ dysfunction caused by infection that ranges in severity. Various systems such as systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) and the quick sequential organ failure assessment (SOFA) have been developed for early detection, but there remains no formal definition. Patients with sepsis often present with hypotension, tachycardia, altered mental status, and organ dysfunction in the setting of a presumed infection. This patient's symptoms share many features with sepsis, but the temporal relationship with transfusion makes TRALI more likely.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The spleen is commonly injured in the setting of blunt abdominal trauma. Injury can present with abdominal tenderness and if severe, hemodynamic instability from large volume hemoperitoneum. Exploratory laparotomy is the best next step in hemodynamically unstable patients or those with severe injuries.</p><p>The spleen is commonly injured in blunt abdominal trauma. Injury patterns include, but are not limited to, devascularization, contusion, laceration, and rupture. The CT scan of the abdomen demonstrates free fluid around the liver and the spleen, likely acute blood products given the clinical history of trauma. The spleen, visualized in the left upper quadrant, is lacerated, indicated by bright extravasation of contrast in areas of organ parenchyma. Splenic laceration and/or rupture can lead to hemorrhage into the peritoneal and retroperitoneal spaces, which can result in hemodynamic instability characterized by tachycardia, and if severe, hypotension. Patients with splenic injuries may have left upper quadrant tenderness, peritonitis from blood products irritating the peritoneum, or referred pain to the left shoulder secondary to diaphragmatic irritation. In hemodynamically stable patients, minor splenic injuries may be treated nonoperatively with observation and reassessment. In unstable patients, exploratory laparotomy is required for repair or splenectomy, depending on the nature and severity of the injury.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Serial examinations in the intensive care unit (Choice A) and repeat CT scan in 24 hours (Choice D) would be appropriate only for stable patients with minor splenic injuries. This patient is tachycardic and critically injured with evidence of active hemorrhage, making this approach inappropriate.</p><p>Angiography (Choice B) can be useful to determine areas of active bleeding and damaged vessels, especially in areas that are not readily available for open exploration through laparotomy, as well as for injury to extremities. Exploratory laparotomy is the most appropriate approach to controlling bleeding from a solid organ injury with extravasation seen on CT scan.</p><p>Ultrasonography (Choice C) can be a helpful adjunct imaging modality in the setting of unstable blunt trauma patients. A focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST) examination can show intraperitoneal free fluid. However, this patient already has had a CT scan with more detail about specific injuries and the presence of free fluid, so a FAST examination would provide no additional diagnostic information.</p><p>Diagnostic peritoneal lavage (Choice E) involves aspiration of peritoneal contents, infusing sterile saline into the peritoneal cavity, and analyzing the effluent. If blood, food particles, bile/bilirubin, amylase, or alkaline phosphatase are found, exploratory laparotomy is indicated as the presence of these findings suggests hollow or solid viscus injury. This patient already received a CT scan demonstrating free fluid, so exploratory laparotomy is the next appropriate step.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neurogenic shock may occur following traumatic brain or spinal cord injury due to disruption of autonomic fibers originating in the vasomotor center within the medulla oblongata. The vasomotor center receives input from baroreceptors in the aortic and carotid sinuses and regulates blood pressure via sympathetic fibers that descend through the lateral aspect of the spinal cord before entering the lateral hom and sympathetic chain ganglia. Loss of sympathetic innervation results in vasodilation and loss of vascular tone.</p><p>Neurogenic shock may occur following traumatic brain or spinal cord injury due to the disruption of autonomic fibers originating in the vasomotor center within the medulla oblongata. The vasomotor center receives input from baroreceptors in the aortic and carotid sinuses and regulates blood pressure via sympathetic fibers that descend through the lateral aspect of the spinal cord before entering the lateral horn and sympathetic chain ganglia. Loss of sympathetic innervation results in vasodilation and loss of vascular tone. Disruption of sympathetic fibers descending from the cardiovascular center in the medulla oblongata may also lead to bradycardia. Careful fluid resuscitation is essential for maintaining perfusion. Atropine may be trialed for the management of bradycardia following acute traumatic spinal cord injury, and vasoactive infusions such as norepinephrine may be necessary to maintain perfusion. Competing diagnoses such as blood loss in the setting of trauma must be excluded or managed if present.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Aortic disruption (Choice A) can lead to rapid loss of intravascular blood volume and shock but is typically accompanied by tachycardia and extravasation of blood into the thorax or abdomen. The absence of fluid on chest x-ray and focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST) renders aortic disruption unlikely.</p><p>Bleeding from the fractures (Choice B) can lead to hemorrhage within the compartments of the lower extremity, but hemodynamically significant hemorrhage is less common with fractures of the tibia and fibula than with the femur. This patient's neurologic findings render neurogenic shock a more likely diagnosis.</p><p>Closed head injury (Choice C) and associated traumatic brain injury can lead to neurogenic shock through disruption of autonomic centers. This patient's mental status and neurologic findings are more suggestive of traumatic spinal cord injury than traumatic brain injury.</p><p>Hepatic injury (Choice D) can lead to significant hemorrhage and shock but would produce free abdominal and pelvic fluid on FAST scan.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is caused by inadequate cerebrospinal fluid resorption and presents with headache, papilledema, and increased opening pressure on lumbar puncture. CT scan is an important initial step in the evaluation to exclude the presence of intracranial masses. If results of the initial CT scan show no abnormalities, lumbar puncture is the most appropriate next step to confirm the presence of increased intracranial pressure.</p><p>Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) is caused by inadequate resorption of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and is characterized by signs of increased intracranial pressure including headache, papilledema, and increased opening pressure on lumbar puncture without gross abnormality on neuroimaging. It is primarily seen in young women with increased BMI. Prolonged increased intracranial pressure may cause chronic headaches and optic nerve damage leading to permanent vision loss. Headaches that are secondary to increased intracranial pressure often worsen when lying down and are relieved by standing. The most common risk factors for the development of IIH include female sex and obesity. Medications associated with IIH include tetracyclines, danazol, and growth hormone. CT scan is an important initial step in the evaluation of a patient with papilledema and suspected IIH to exclude the presence of an intracranial mass. If the CT scan is normal, lumbar puncture is the most appropriate next step to confirm the presence of increased intracranial pressure. Management of IIH includes weight reduction, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, therapeutic lumbar puncture, and in rare cases, surgical intervention with cerebrospinal fluid shunting or optic nerve sheath fenestration.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Optical coherence tomography (Choice A) allows for objective measurement of optic nerve swelling. It is useful for establishing the presence of papilledema when the clinical examination is equivocal, or for monitoring the resolution of papilledema during treatment. It is not as useful for establishing the diagnosis of IIH as lumbar puncture.</p><p>Intravenous administration of methylprednisolone (Choice B) is useful for the acute management of optic neuritis, which generally presents with acute onset vision loss and ocular pain that is exacerbated with extraocular movements. Patients with optic neuritis typically present with decreased visual acuity and color vision, along with a relative afferent pupillary defect. Optic neuritis may present in young females, commonly in association with multiple sclerosis. Steroids do not play a role in the management of IIH.</p><p>Intravenous administration of nifedipine (Choice C) is useful for the management of optic disc edema secondary to hypertension, Hypertensive retinopathy commonly occurs in patients with severe, uncontrolled hypertension. Disc edema caused by increased intracranial pressure must be distinguished from other causes of optic disc swelling. Lumbar puncture should be performed to exclude the presence of increased intracranial pressure prior to attributing the patient's optic disc swelling to hypertension.</p><p>Visual evoked potentials, or VEP (Choice D), involves the placement of electrodes on the scalp to measure the electrical response of the visual cortex in response to standardized visual stimuli, typically an alternating black and white checkerboard pattern or a series of single, bright flashes of light. VEPs are useful for the assessment of patients with optic nerve conduction delay, such as in the setting of multiple sclerosis, or for the evaluation of ocular albinism or suspected non-organic vision loss.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is the most common form of skin cancer and presents as pink, pearly papules or nodules with rolled borders and central ulceration most commonly on the sun-exposed areas of the head and neck. In all subtypes, the risk of metastasis is low, though the tumors can invade locally and cause significant tissue damage. Treatment of BCC requires surgical removal, either via excision or Mohs micrographic surgery.</p><p>Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is the most common form of skin cancer and is derived from the basal cells of the epidermis. The primary risk factor for developing basal cell carcinoma is ultraviolet light exposure, though age, fair skin, and family history also contribute. BCC typically presents as pink, pearly papules or nodules with rolled borders and central ulceration commonly on the sun-exposed areas of the head and neck. Subtypes of BCC include those that are less invasive, such as superficial and nodular, and those that are more invasive, such as infiltrative and morpheaform. In all subtypes, the risk of metastasis is low, though the tumors can invade locally and cause significant tissue damage. Biopsy may be performed with a variety of techniques, including excisional, shave, or punch biopsy. Treatment of BCC requires complete surgical removal, either via excision or Mohs micrographic surgery.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Actinic keratosis (Choice A) is a premalignant lesion that may progress to squamous cell carcinoma, Clinically, lesions typically appear as light pink, ill-defined macules with a gritty texture in areas of prolonged sun exposure, such as the face, ears, and dorsal hands. The presence of central ulceration in this patient's lesion is more suggestive of BCC.</p><p>Hemangioma (Choice C) presents as a firm, raised, highly vascular lesion. Hemangiomas typically present in childhood and are rare in elderly patients.</p><p>Melanoma (Choice D) presents as a pigmented skin lesion, typically demonstrating asymmetry, iregular appearing borders, variable coloration, a diameter greater than 6 mm, and rapid evolution in characteristics. Subtypes include superficial spreading, nodular, lentigo maligna, and acral lentiginous. Amelanotic melanoma, in which no pigment is present, may appear initially similar to a BCC, but is less common.</p><p>Squamous cell carcinoma (Choice E) can often be differentiated from basal cell carcinoma based on appearance alone. Squamous cell carcinoma tends to have a hyperkeratotic crust rather than a smooth, pearly surface. However, pathologic diagnosis is needed to definitively differentiate the two skin cancers.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is the most common form of skin cancer and presents as pink, pearly papules or nodules with rolled borders and central ulceration most commonly on the sun-exposed areas of the head and neck. Complete surgical excision with a margin of between 5 mm to 1 cm is recommended as first line treatment for BCC located in noncritical areas that are amenable to complete resection with adequate margins.</p><p>Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is the most common form of skin cancer and is derived from the basal cells of the epidermis. The primary risk factor for developing BCC is ultraviolet light exposure, though age, fair skin, and family history also contribute. BCC typically presents as pink, pearly papules or nodules with rolled borders and central ulceration commonly on the sun-exposed areas of the head and neck. Subtypes of BCC include those that are less invasive, such as superficial and nodular, and those that are more invasive, such as infiltrative and morpheaform. In all subtypes, the risk of metastasis is low, though the tumors can invade locally and cause significant tissue damage. Complete surgical excision with a margin of between 5 mm to 1 cm is recommended as first line treatment for BCC located in noncritical areas that are amenable to complete resection with adequate margins. In critical areas of the face or other delicate regions, Mohs micrographic surgery may allow for complete excision with comparative sparing of normal tissues.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Excision with 2-cm margins (Choice B) or 5-cm margins (Choice C) is not necessary for the treatment of BCC. The risk of overall metastasis is low with BCC, and the risk of recurrence with 1 cm margins is less than 1%. More extensive margins may be employed for the primary resection of more aggressive lesions, such as cutaneous melanoma.</p><p>Sentinel lymph node biopsy (Choices D, E, and F) is not usually recommended for most patients with BCC. Metastatic BCC is rare, and the likely yield of sentinel lymph node biopsy does not justify its routine performance.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Management of mild frostbite injuries involves rewarming by immersing the affected area in warm water, analgesia, and admission to the hospital for observation and wound care.</p><p>Frostbite is frequently seen in patients who work in the cold, mountaineers and skiers, individuals stranded outdoors, and those experiencing homelessness. Frostbite causes tissue destruction via cold-induced cell death, as well as reperfusion-related inflammation and tissue ischemia. There are several ways to classify the severity of frostbite, depending on degree of cyanosis, depth of tissue involvement, blistering, or findings on technetium-99m imaging. Immediate management of frostbite involves removing cold or wet clothing, transferring the patient to a warm environment, rapid rewarming by immersing the affected area in warm (but not hot) water, analgesia, and admission to the hospital for observation and wound care. Several adjunct therapies are often employed to limit tissue damage, including thrombolysis. Initial signs and symptoms of frostbite are often unreliable in determining the extent of injury; the clinical picture evolves as occult damage becomes apparent.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Application of nitroglycerin ointment (Choice B) is not as effective as immersion into warm water or warm compresses to help with vasodilation. Nitroglycerin has also been used in facilitating treatment with intra-arterial thrombolysis, but it is not the next most appropriate step in management.</p><p>Intravenous infusion of unfractionated heparin (Choice C) alone has not been shown to improve outcomes in patients with frostbite. Intravenous heparin can be used as an adjunct to intravenous thrombolysis, but it would not be an appropriate next step in this patient.</p><p>Immediate arteriography of the lower extremities (Choice D) would be indicated in patients with acute limb ischemia with loss of pulses. It would not be appropriate in this patient with palpable pedal pulses.</p><p>Amputation of all toes with blisters at 24 hours post injury (Choice E) is not immediately indicated and depends on viability of tissue after observation, adequate treatment, and rewarming in warm water. Blisters may be a sign of nonviable tissue though may be present in otherwise warm, well-perfused tissue as a response to initial injury.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Peripheral arterial disease may lead to chronic, progressive lower extremity ischemia that can cause gangrene when tissue perfusion becomes inadequate to meet metabolic demand. Dry gangrene is most common in patients with peripheral arterial disease, although wet gangrene may occur. Revascularization surgery may allow for salvage of the limb and improve rest pain or non-healing wounds, but the presence of gangrene generally requires amputation.</p><p>Peripheral arterial disease (PAD) refers to the abnormally diminished flow of blood to the extremities secondary to atherosclerotic plaque. This most commonly affects the lower extremities. Risk factors include diabetes mellitus, increased age, tobacco smoking, hypertension, and dyslipidemia. A known history of atherosclerosis (eg, coronary artery disease, cerebrovascular disease) identifies the likelihood of the condition being present in other locations as well. Patients with PAD typically present with intermittent claudication, which results from insufficient blood flow to the muscles during exercise. As the flow of blood becomes progressively diminished with worsening atherosclerosis, tissue perfusion may become inadequate to meet metabolic demands, and can present with pain at rest and the potential for ischemia and necrosis (gangrene) of the extremity. Dry gangrene is most common in patients with PAD, although wet gangrene may also occur. Revascularization surgery may allow for salvage of the limb and improve rest pain or non-healing wounds, but the presence of gangrene generally requires amputation. In this case, an immobile patient with contractures will generally not suffer functional loss from amputation, making it a suitable choice if it aligns with goals of care.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Cilostazol therapy (Choice A) causes vasodilation and is useful for the treatment of claudication due to PAD. While it leads to improvement in claudication and in walking distance, it is inadequate for the treatment of severe PAD with secondary gangrene.</p><p>Arteriography with contrast (Choice B) is useful for the assessment of vascular stenosis in patients with PAD or in patients with suspected acute limb ischemia. Surgical amputation is a more important next step in this patient with absent pulses and lower extremity gangrene.</p><p>Percutaneous balloon angioplasty (Choice C) and surgical revascularization (Choice D) with stenting or atherectomy are options for revascularization in patients with PAD. The presence of gangrene is an indication for amputation, as the limb is often not salvageable with revascularization.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Malignancies of bone commonly occur in the lower extremities and present with chronic, often localized bone pain and a palpable bony or soft tissue lump or protuberance. This patient's x-ray demonstrates medullary and cortical bone destruction of the anterior tibia with a periosteal reaction, indicating possible malignancy. A biopsy of the lesion is the most important next step in management and can be performed with core needle or open biopsy technique.</p><p>Malignancies of bone commonly occur in the lower extremities and present with chronic, often localized bone pain and a palpable bony or soft tissue lump or protuberance. A minority of patients may present with fracture of the affected bone. X-rays are an important step in diagnosis. This patient's x-ray demonstrates medullary and cortical bone destruction of the anterior, distal, medial tibia, and malleolus with a periosteal reaction, indicating possible malignancy. A biopsy of the lesion is the most important next step in management and can be performed with core needle or open biopsy technique.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Nonweight-bearing exercises (Choice A) is inappropriate and would result in a delay in diagnosis of a suspected bone malignancy.</p><p>Nonweight-bearing exercises (Choice A) is inappropriate and would result in a delay in diagnosis of a suspected bone malignancy.</p><p>Second x-ray in 2 months (Choice B) is unnecessary and will result in a delay in diagnosis and treatment. The current x-ray demonstrates bony destruction and periosteal reaction, which warrants prompt biopsy as the next step.</p><p>Bone scan (Choice C) and DEXA scan (Choice D) are useful for the diagnosis of osteoporosis. Bone scanning may be useful for assessment of distant bone metastases. However, MRI is the preferred radiographic modality for characterization of the primary lesion. Biopsy should be performed prior to any further radiographic testing.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Perioperative cardiovascular complications are a significant cause of morbidity and mortality. Preoperative risk assessment with a validated tool can help patients and clinicians make informed decisions prior to surgical procedures.</p><p>Perioperative cardiovascular complications, referred to as major adverse cardiovascular and cerebrovascular events (MACCE), are a significant cause of morbidity and mortality in noncardiac surgical cases. Anesthesia and surgery place the body under tremendous physiological stress, which can lead to myocardial ischemia, infarction, and potential cardiac death. This patient has several cardiac risk factors, including a history of coronary artery disease, and currently presents with abdominal pain, anxiety, diaphoresis, and tachycardia. A 12-lead ECG and serum troponin level should be obtained immediately to evaluate for acute coronary syndrome. The risk of MACCE occurring can be determined by use of a validated cardiac risk assessment tool (such as the revised cardiac risk index (RCRI) or the American College of Surgeons National Surgical Quality Improvement Program (ACS NSQIP) surgical risk calculator). In general, the patient's functional status prior to surgery is often the best indicator of perioperative cardiovascular risk.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Serum d-dimer assay (Choice A) is a nonspecific test used to assess for active thromboembolic disease, such as a pulmonary embolism. It is likely to be increased in the setting of recent gastrointestinal hemorrhage, and surgery and would not provide any useful information for this patient. If pulmonary embolism were considered, pulmonary vascular imaging would be the best next step.</p><p>CT scan of the abdomen (Choice C) may be considered given the patient's abdominal pain and recent surgery, as surgical site infections and dehiscence are potential complications. However, his symptoms are also indicative of acute coronary syndrome, and a 12-lead ECG should be performed first.</p><p>Upper endoscopy (Choice D) is not likely to be useful in the immediate diagnostic work-up of this patient. It may be needed if the patient presents with recurrent bleeding or complications involving the gastric pouch; however, an ECG, laboratory studies, and imaging studies should be performed first.</p><p>Local wound exploration at bedside (Choice E) is unlikely to disclose further information than the physical examination, given that there is little evidence for infection, as the patient is afebrile, does not exhibit leukocytosis, and does not demonstrate extensive erythema or drainage from the surgical site.</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with severe, symptomatic high gradient aortic stenosis, decreased exercise tolerance, or a left ventricular ejection fraction less than 50% benefit from aortic valve replacement, which may be performed surgically or through a transcatheter approach.</p><p>Aortic stenosis (AS) is a common disorder of the aortic valve that results from calcification. Calcification of the aortic valve is a pathologic consequence of mechanical stresses on heart valves, and results from repetitive microtrauma from the opening and closing of valve leaflets with associated chronic inflammation. While many patients may be asymptomatic, those with severe AS may complain of fatigue, shortness of breath, cough, diminished exercise tolerance, angina, or syncope with exertion. Due to the chronic increased afterload from a fixed obstruction by the valve, left ventricular hypertrophy and resultant diastolic dysfunction can occur. If untreated, complications of aortic stenosis include heart failure, secondary pulmonary hypertension, and potential sudden cardiac death. Angina and syncope result from inadequate end-organ perfusion (of the myocardium and brain respectively), and the presence of these symptoms in the setting of AS is an indication for aortic valve replacement.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Generalized weakness and fatigue (Choice B) are nonspecific, common symptoms in AS and alone do not warrant a valve replacement procedure. Similarly, peripheral edema and ascites (Choice C) may develop as a consequence of chronic heart failure related to AS, but do not directly indicate impaired perfusion to essential organs.</p><p>Pulmonary embolus (Choice D) is not directly associated with aortic stenosis, although the stenotic valve can potentially cause increased left ventricular strain, pulmonary congestion, and impaired systemic perfusion.</p><p>Ventricular arrhythmia (Choice E) is common in patients with AS, especially when left ventricular systolic or diastolic dysfunction is present. It is not considered an independent indication for aortic valve replacement.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute limb ischemia is a severe, limb-threatening complication of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease and presents with acute pain, pallor, and pulselessness of a lower extremity. Management includes anticoagulation, analgesia, and surgical intervention.</p><p>Peripheral arterial disease (PAD) refers to the abnormally diminished flow of blood to the extremities secondary to atherosclerotic plaque. This most commonly affects the lower extremities. Risk factors include diabetes mellitus, increased age, tobacco smoking, hypertension, and dyslipidemia. Patients typically present with claudication caused by insufficient blood flow to the muscles during exercise, as is described in this patient's history. As the flow of blood becomes progressively diminished with worsening atherosclerosis, tissue perfusion may become inadequate to meet metabolic demands. In acute cases of limb ischemia, patients generally present with pain, pallor, paresthesia, loss of temperature sensation, paralysis, and pulselessness. Acute limb ischemia can complicate the state of already diminished vascular supply in PAD (such as when an existing plaque ruptures in- situ leading to thrombotic acute limb ischemia), or when an embolic source abruptly occludes an already narrowed vessel (such as in cases of atrial fibrillation). Atrial fibrillation, when suboptimally anticoagulated as in this case (the patient's risk factors merit initiation of a blood thinner beyond aspirin to prevent left atrial thrombosis and risk for thromboembolism) can result in distal embolization of thrombi. This leads to strokes, acute limb ischemia, mesenteric or renal ischemia, acute coronary syndromes, or other phenomena of end-organ hypoperfusion depending on the location of embolized clot. Acute management of thromboembolism includes anticoagulation, analgesics, and reperfusion procedures (eg, vascular bypass, endovascular or open thrombectomy).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D and E.</p><p>Compartment syndrome (Choice A) presents with pain, paresthesia, paresis, pallor, and pulselessness. It occurs because of increased pressure from swelling or hemorrhage within a fixed-space myofascial compartment. When the pressure approaches or exceeds diastolic blood pressure, blood flow becomes impaired. Swelling of the extremity and palpation of a tense compartment is typical. It can occur without preceding trauma, though this is less likely.</p><p>Deep venous thrombosis (Choice B) presents with pain and swelling (pitting edema), typically involving one leg, often with a palpable superficial venous cord, and with pain when the extremity is passively flexed at the ankle (the ‘Homan’ sign). Rather than presenting with pallor (except in the case of phlegmasia alba dolens), the affected extremity is usually swollen with a ruddy or violaceous hue.</p><p>Rest pain secondary to progressive atherosclerotic peripheral vascular disease (Choice D) commonly occurs as a natural progression of claudication secondary to peripheral arterial disease. However, it is unlikely to present acutely with pain, pallor, and absent pulses, all of which are more suggestive of an embolic etiology.</p><p>Thrombosis of the left superficial femoral artery (Choice E) may occur as a result of progressive peripheral arterial disease and may result in limb-threatening ischemia. However, this patient's history of acute onset symptoms in the setting of new, suboptimally-controlled atrial fibrillation is more consistent with arterial embolism.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients who develop a DVT should be treated with anticoagulant medications and assessed for the presence of risk factors, Recent surgery may be considered a relative contraindication to anticoagulation, and a careful assessment of bleeding risk should be performed. Anticoagulation with heparin may be preferable for select patients, particularly those with a history of recent surgery or malignancy, because of the greater ease of reversal of anticoagulation with heparin.</p><p>Leg swelling following recent surgery and known malignancy may provoke deep venous thrombosis (DVT). DVT, a thrombus within the deep veins of the body, generally the leg, is generally provoked by the features of Virchow's triad: venous stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability. In this case, risk factors such as surgery, malignancy, and prolonged immobilization likely contributed to the development of a DVT. While the distinction of provoked or unprovoked DVT matters in deciding long-term anticoagulation, in the immediate setting, the management is the same regardless of the reason for occurrence, and anticoagulation is the appropriate best next step. Recent surgery may be considered a relative contraindication to anticoagulation, and a careful assessment of bleeding risk should be performed. Clinical calculators, such as the HAS-BLED score, can be useful for quantifying the risk of bleeding with anticoagulation. Anticoagulation with heparin may be preferable for select patients, particularly those with a history of recent surgery or malignancy, due to greater ease of reversal of anticoagulation with heparin (short half-life, readily available reversal agent), compared to other agents.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Elevation of the lower extremity (Choice A) may alleviate discomfort and swelling associated with DVT but is not a substitute for anticoagulation.</p><p>Use of compression stockings (Choice B) is often employed in patients with DVT but may be uncomfortable and lacks proven benefit. It would not take priority over anticoagulation to prevent propagation or embolization of a thrombus.</p><p>Physical therapy (Choice C) may be useful in promoting early ambulation and is not known to increase the risk of embolization, but anticoagulation would take priority.</p><p>Intravenous streptokinase therapy (Choice D) is a thrombolytic agent that may be used in extensive or proximal DVT or venous thromboembolism. Patients with uncomplicated DVT do not usually require thrombolysis. Additionally, high risk of bleeding (such as due to recent surgery) is a contraindication to administration of thrombolytics.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: BCI is occasionally seen after MVAs when patients suffer direct blunt trauma to the precordium or undergo rapid deceleration. The clinical presentation ranges from the development of mild arrhythmias to life-threatening ventricular or valvular rupture leading to pericardial tamponade and cardiogenic shock. Patients with new arrhythmias following BCI should be admitted for cardiac monitoring.</p><p>Blunt cardiac injury (BCI) is the most likely diagnosis in this patient who recently sustained injuries in a motor vehicle accident (MVA) and now has evidence of an irregular cardiac rhythm. BCI is most often seen in the setting of MVAs, and the incidence is higher in those who sustain blunt trauma directly to the precordium or suffer rapid deceleration. BCI can result in a wide range of cardiac injuries from mild to life-threatening. Arrhythmias following BCI occur in approximately 5% of patients, and rhythm abnormalities range from premature ventricular contractions to supraventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation. In most instances, the arrhythmias are mild and not life-threatening, although all patients should be admitted for cardiac monitoring. More severe complications of BCI include ventricular rupture leading to pericardial tamponade, valvular rupture, and myocardial infarction secondary to coronary artery dissection or thrombosis, Patients who suffer catastrophic injuries typically do not survive transport to the emergency department. Specific cardiac abnormalities, including cardiac tamponade and valvular pathologies, can usually be diagnosed using echocardiography and other clinical features such as refractory hypotension, pulsus paradoxus, and jugular venous distension.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Cardiomyopathy (Choice B) is a broad term that refers to dysfunction of the cardiac muscle that may arise from numerous causes. Several varieties of cardiomyopathy include dilated, hypertrophic, stress-induced, and restrictive. Patients present with myocardial dysfunction, which may or may not be associated with arrhythmias, but this patient's presentation is more consistent with BCI.</p><p>Cor pulmonale (Choice C) refers to right ventricular dysfunction that results from chronic pulmonary pathology and associated pulmonary hypertension. Chronically increased pulmonary pressures lead to right ventricular remodeling and failure over time. Patients often present with exertional dyspnea and hypoxemia. This patient's acute presentation and mechanism of injury suggest BCI as the cause of her arrhythmia.</p><p>Hypovolemic shock (Choice D) should always be suspected as the most likely cause of unexplained sinus tachycardia and hypotension in a trauma patient. This patient appears otherwise stable and has developed a new irregular cardiac rhythm after blunt trauma to the chest, which is more consistent with BCl-induced arrhythmia rather than hypovolemic shock.</p><p>Pericardial tamponade (Choice E) can occur as a result of ventricular rupture from BCI, but patients present with tachycardia, hypotension, pulsus paradoxus, jugular venous distention, and muffled heart sounds. This patient does not demonstrate any of these features.</p>", "16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cor pulmonale refers to isolated right heart failure secondary to chronic pulmonary pathology with increased pulmonary vascular resistance and right-sided heart pressures. Patients with untreated OSA are at risk for developing pulmonary hypertension which, if untreated, may lead to cor pulmonale.</p><p>If this patient remains untreated for his obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), he is at risk of developing cor pulmonale. OSA is characterized by intermittent obstruction of the upper airway leading to hypoxia and apnea. A history of snoring, followed by periods of witnessed apnea in association with daytime somnolence or moming headaches, is common. The severity of OSA is determined using the apnea-hypopnea index (AHI), and treatment of moderate to severe OSA is with the use of a continuous positive ainvay pressure (CPAP) device at night. The pressure supplied by a CPAP keeps the upper ainvay open, thereby preventing upper airway obstruction and apneic episodes. Severe, untreated OSA can lead to a variety of complications, including pulmonary hypertension. As the pulmonary circulation is usually a low-pressure system, the right ventricle is not equipped to pump against such increased pressures. Thus, pulmonary hypertension causes secondary right ventricular dysfunction, referred to as cor pulmonale. Cor pulmonale manifests in findings of right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy on ECG and chest x-ray with peripheral edema and hepatomegaly (signs of right-sided heart failure). The enlarged right ventricle is associated with a precordial heave on examination and a pronounced S,, given the increased pressure of the pulmonary system impacting the closure dynamics of the pulmonic valve. This patient has evidence of right ventricular dysfunction and should be treated with CPAP at night to prevent chronic hypoxia, pulmonary vasoconstriction, and irreversible right ventricular remodeling and failure.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Aortic dissection (Choice A) usually presents with severe chest pain that radiates to the back. Examination findings vary depending upon whether the dissection flap travels retrograde toward the aortic root, in which case patients may develop acute aortic regurgitation or coronary occlusion, or if the dissection flap travels anterograde toward the descending aorta, in which case patients may develop a pulse deficit between the two arms along with signs of distal ischemia or end-organ damage.</p><p>Intracerebral hemorrhage (Choice C) presents with numerous possible symptoms depending upon the extent and location of hemorrhage, but patients often present with headaches and focal neurologic deficits. This patient's presentation is inconsistent with this diagnosis.</p><p>Iron deficiency anemia (Choice D) may lead to fatigue and exertional dyspnea if severe. If undiagnosed for a prolonged period of time, severe anemia can occasionally lead to high output heart failure, but this is rare. This patient's presentation is not consistent with this diagnosis.</p><p>Thrombotic stroke (Choice E) usually presents with a cadence of worsening neurologic symptoms depending upon the rapidity with which the thrombosis develops and its location. This patient does not have any focal neurologic deficits to suggest a stroke.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute PE classically presents with pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea, and hypoxemia. Several clinical criteria have been identified that increase the pretest probability of a PE. The Wells’ Criteria include tachycardia, surgery within the past 4 weeks, lack of a clear alternative diagnosis, evidence of a current DVT, prior history of DVT and/or PE, hemoptysis, and history of malignancy within the past 6 months,</p><p>The patient's presentation is conceming for acute pulmonary embolism (PE), and heparin therapy should be initiated immediately. PE can be difficult to diagnose as it often presents with nonspecific and varied symptoms. Several validated clinical decision-making aids are used in practice, such as the Wells’ Criteria. In a patient presenting with acute dyspnea, the presence of tachycardia, history of recent surgery within the past 4 weeks, and lack of a clear alternative diagnosis for his symptoms place him at a higher risk for acute PE, and his case should be managed accordingly. The Wells’ Criteria also include clinical evidence of a current deep venous thrombosis (DVT), prior history of DVT and/or PE, hemoptysis, and presence of malignancy within the past 6 months. Other symptoms and signs that are commonly associated with acute PE include pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea, and hypoxemia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Reassurance (Choice A) at this point would not be appropriate. The pretest probability of acute PE given the patient's presentation is high, and initiating anticoagulation is indicated while further diagnostic workup proceeds.</p><p>Aspirin therapy (Choice B), and antiplatelet therapy in general, alone is not adequate for the management of acute PE. Intravenous unfractionated heparin is preferred in the initial management because of its rapid onset and clearance, along with inhibition of the coagulation cascade.</p><p>Metronidazole therapy (Choice D) would be considered if the patient’s symptoms were best explained by an intra-abdominal infection, in addition to an antibiotic with broad enteric gram-negative bacterial coverage. While he does have a recent history of diverticular abscess, his operation was for source control of that infection, and he is currently afebrile with several risk factors for acute PE.</p><p>Transfusion of 2 units of packed red blood cells (Choice E) is not indicated. The patient is normotensive and does not demonstrate evidence of symptomatic anemia on physical examination. An intra-abdominal bleed related to his recent surgery is less likely in the absence of abdominal pain and tenderness.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypercapnia that is not compensated (as indicated by acidemia) and hypoxemia in the setting of respiratory distress indicate respiratory failure. Advanced airway interventions are indicated to support the vital respiratory functions of oxygenation and ventilation.</p><p>The arterial blood gas (ABG) indicates the patient is experiencing hypoxemic and hypercapnic respiratory failure with clear clinical evidence of respiratory distress. The most appropriate next step is intubation and mechanical ventilation for respiratory support, regardless of the determined etiology of respiratory failure, as this patient requires immediate management of an acute life threat. Perioperative pulmonary complications are a major cause of mortality and morbidity in surgical patients. Thoracic and abdominal surgery reduce vital capacity and functional residual capacity significantly, due to diaphragm dysfunction, splinting, and postoperative pain. Other risk factors for perioperative pulmonary complications include age, obesity, obstructive sleep apnea, asthma, COPD, or pulmonary hypertension, and general nutritional and functional status.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, and G. Administration of a fluid bolus (Choice A) is likely to be counterproductive in this situation because of the risk for pulmonary edema worsening the patient's hypoxemia and gas</p><p>indicates the patient is experiencing hypoxemic and hypercapnic respiratory failure with clear clinical evidence of respiratory appropriate next step is intubation and mechanical ventilation for respiratory support, regardless of the determined etiology of respiratory failure, as this patient requires immediate management of an acute life threat. Perioperative pulmonary complications are a major cause of mortality and morbidity in surgical patients. Thoracic and abdominal surgery reduce vital capacity and functional residual capacity significantly, due to diaphragm dysfunction, splinting, and postoperative pain. Other risk factors for perioperative pulmonary complications include age, obesity, obstructive sleep apnea, asthma, COPD, or pulmonary hypertension, and general nutritional and functional status.</p><p>Administration of a fluid bolus (Choice A) is likely to be counterproductive in this situation because of the risk for pulmonary edema worsening the patient's hypoxemia and gas exchange. The patient is normotensive, and tachycardia is better explained as a compensatory response to decreased oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood from impaired gas exchange.</p><p>Administration of naloxone (Choice B) may be considered, especially if the patient has received opioid analgesics in the perioperative period. However, given the respiratory failure indicated by her respiratory distress, tachypnea, respiratory acidosis due to hypercapnia, and hypoxemia, intubation is the priority in management to provide appropriate oxygenation and ventilatory support. Opioid overdoses present with pinpoint pupils and bradypnea, not tachypnea and agitation; naloxone is not indicated.</p><p>Diuretic therapy (Choice C) may be considered as an adjunct therapy, especially if significant volumes of intravenous fluid were administered during the case, or if chest imaging post-intubation reveals evidence of pulmonary edema.</p><p>Fiberoptic bronchoscopy (Choice D) is a valuable diagnostic and therapeutic tool for patients with severe respiratory illness, especially if the etiology is unknown or biopsy collection is indicated. In this case, the etiology of the patient's respiratory failure is multifactorial in the perioperative setting and complicated by obesity; intubation takes priority, and a fiberoptic bronchoscopy is unlikely to disclose any further information necessary for management at this time. Similarly, laryngoscopy (Choice G) is used to evaluate for laryngeal lesions and vocal cord dysfunction, which is not helpful in the acute management of the patient's respiratory failure.</p><p>Heparin therapy (Choice E) would be indicated if the etiology of the patient's respiratory failure was acute pulmonary embolism or acute coronary syndrome, which are both complications to consider in the perioperative period. However, the current management priority is respiratory support with intubation, and the history is more suggestive of perioperative hypoventilation as the cause of the patient's symptoms.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypoventilation can be identified by decreased Po2, increased Pco2, and a normal A-a gradient on ABG. Common etiologies include obesity hypoventilation syndrome, central nervous system depressants, and neuromuscular disorders affecting the diaphragm.</p><p>Hypoventilation can be identified by decreased Po2, increased Pco., and a normal alveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient on arterial blood gas analysis (ABG). In this case, the pH indicates a mild acidemia, the Pco2 shows hypercapnia, and the Po2 is at the lower range of normal. The A-a gradient can be estimated as a function of atmospheric pressure, Fio, Po2 and Pco2. Adjusting for the normal increase in A-a gradient associated with age, the patient's A-a gradient falls within an expected range. This suggests the etiology of his hypercapnic respiratory failure is secondary to decreased ventilation, which aligns with his history of obesity (BMI 52 kg/m2) and auscultation of decreased breath sounds bilaterally. To determine the acuity of his respiratory acidosis, an estimate can be made comparing the amount of decrease in his pH with the associated increase in Pco.. For acute respiratory acidosis, the expected change in pH = 0.008 x (Pco- 40). For chronic respiratory acidosis, the expected change in pH = 0.003 x (Pco2- 40). In this case, the observed change in PH is ~0.09. This falls between the expected values for an acute (change of 0.13) or chronic (change of 0.05) respiratory acidosis; thus, the appropriate conclusion is that this represents an acute on chronic respiratory acidosis, which also agrees with the diagnosis of hypoventilation secondary to obesity,</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, E, F, and G.</p><p>Decreased diffusion capacity (Choice A) is a useful indicator of parenchymal lung disease, in which the underlying alveolar architecture is disrupted leading to decreased total surface area for gas exchange. Hypoventilation results in a decreased volume of gas exchange, with no disruption of diffusion or perfusion.</p><p>Increased diffusion capacity (Choice C), greater than expected, is most often seen in cases of large lung volumes, obesity, and some variants of asthma. It would not account for this patient's symptoms and ABG findings.</p><p>Increased ventilation (Choice D) is supported by an ABG that shows a decreased Pco2. Hyperventilation in the acute setting causes a respiratory alkalosis, Left-to-right shunting (Choice E) occurs when there is an abnormal passage between the systemic and pulmonary systems, with the higher systemic blood pressure shunting blood flow into the pulmonary system. Atrial and ventricular septal defects are common causes, and severe defects can result in progressive pulmonary hypertension and the subsequent development of right-to-left shunting (Choice F) over time, also known as Eisenmenger syndrome. Right-to-left shunting presents with hypoxemia and an increased A-a gradient.</p><p>Ventilation-perfusion mismatch (Choice G) refers to an imbalance in the amount of blood perfusing alveoli where gas exchange can take place. Due to the mismatch, the A-a gradient is generally increased.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In blunt trauma, diaphragm rupture can occur due to a sudden increase in intraabdominal pressure. A traumatic diaphragmatic hernia can lead to the presence of abdominal contents within the thorax, and presents with chest pain, shortness of breath, and bowel sounds within the lung fields.</p><p>Traumatic diaphragmatic hernia can occur after penetrating injury or blunt trauma, which is caused by a sudden increase in intraabdominal pressure that leads to rupture of the diaphragm. An opening in the diaphragm can lead to the presence of abdominal contents within the thorax, and presents with chest pain, shortness of breath, and bowel sounds within the lung fields. It commonly occurs in the left hemithorax and may be visualized as intestinal loops or other abdominal organs in the thorax on x-ray. In this patient's chest x-ray, there is a gastric bubble visible in the left hemithorax. Other chest x-ray findings can include elevation of the hemidiaphragm, abnormal nasogastric tube positioning, atelectasis, or blunting of normal architecture. Management includes surgical exploration and repair of the ruptured diaphragm.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, E, F, and G.</p><p>Aortic rupture (Choice A) can also occur in deceleration injuries. Patients can present with chest pain, hypotension, tachycardia, and a widened mediastinum on chest x-ray. Elevation of hemidiaphragm and intestines visualized in the thorax is more consistent with a ruptured diaphragm.</p><p>Flail chest (Choice C) generally results when two or more adjacent ribs are fractured at two or more locations, which results in paradoxical movement of that segment of the chest wall. in tha ntnural nanan aftan fram trauma ta tha nulmanans hranahial arintaranctal uncentic in tha eating af ch feanturne LUnmatharay [Phainn DV caniilie fram bland annotating ui</p><p>Hemothorax (Choice D) results from blood accumulating within the pleural space, often from trauma to the pulmonary, bronchial, or intercostal vessels in the setting of rib fractures or penetrating trauma. It presents with chest pain, shortness of breath, dullness to percussion, and diminished breath sounds on the affected side. If massive, hemodynamic instability may occur from blood loss or tension on great vessels.</p><p>Pneumothorax (Choice E) can appear on chest x-ray as radiolucent air and the absence of interstitial lung markings in the pleural space adjacent to the lung parenchyma. The displacement of the lung in this patient is due to radiopaque tissue, not radiolucent air.</p><p>Pulmonary contusion (Choice F) may occur after blunt or penetrating trauma to the chest. They often underlie the region of trauma to the chest wall and result from damage to capillaries with associated leakage of intravascular and extravascular fluid into the pulmonary interstitium and alveolar airspaces, respectively. On chest x-rays, pulmonary contusions appear as interstitial infiltrates and/or patchy alveolar opacities.</p><p>Tracheal laceration (Choice G) may result from blunt or penetrating trauma to the chest or neck, and generally presents with acute shortness of breath, abnormal breath sounds or stridor, hemoptysis, hypoxia, and respiratory distress, depending on the severity. Air would be present in the mediastinum or neck on imaging.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Common causes of postoperative fever are atelectasis, pneumonia, urinary tract infection, infections of the surgical site, or intrathoracic or intraabdominal infections related to the surgery, among other etiologies. To evaluate for intraabdominal abscess, CT scan with contrast should be performed.</p><p>Postoperative complications can include infection, bleeding, wound dehiscence, and thromboembolic disease, among others. Common causes of postoperative fever are atelectasis, pneumonia, urinary tract infection, infections of the surgical site, or intrathoracic or intraabdominal infections related to the surgery. Thromboembolic disease with deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism can also present with a fever, and bacteremia can also cause fever. In this patient, the most likely cause of fever is due to an intraabdominal or surgical site abscess causing fever, leukocytosis with a left shift, and abdominal fullness in the left upper quadrant at the site of the splenectomy. To diagnose an intraabdominal abscess, a CT scan of the abdomen with contrast should be performed. Management involves intravenous antibiotics and, in some cases, percutaneous or operative drainage.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Ultrasonography of the gallbladder (Choice A) can be helpful in diagnosing cholelithiasis and cholecystitis, which would cause right upper quadrant pain. This patient's left upper quadrant fullness is unlikely to be due to gallbladder pathology.</p><p>Upper gastrointestinal series (Choice B) is used when structural lesions involving the stomach or small bowel are suspected. This is most indicated for the evaluation of small bowel pathology, such as duodenal atresia, malrotation with volvulus, and Crohn disease.</p><p>Liver-spleen scan (Choice D) is a nuclear medicine test used to evaluate for pathologies such as cysts, abscesses, and malignancies in the liver and spleen. However, it is a time- intensive test that is not readily available and would not be appropriate in the acute care of this patient.</p><p>Bronchoscopy (Choice E) allows for the direct visualization of the airway in the diagnosis and management of infection, mucus plugging, lung masses, and foreign body aspirations.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Crohn disease is a subclass of inflammatory bowel disease that causes transmural inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract and may involve any region from the mouth to the anus. Patients often present with chronic abdominal pain, diarrhea, occult gastrointestinal bleeding, and occasionally with extraintestinal manifestations. The presence of anal fistulas in patients with corresponding gastrointestinal symptoms strongly supports the diagnosis.</p><p>Crohn disease is the most likely diagnosis in this young patient with a low BMI, positive fecal occult blood test, diarrhea with mucus, and the presence of an anal fistula on examination. Crohn disease is characterized by transmural inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract, most frequently involving the terminal ileum. It can arise anywhere in the gastrointestinal tract and is a subclass of inflammatory bowel disease. Most patients experience chronic abdominal cramping, bloody diarrhea, and weight loss. Examination often demonstrates generalized abdominal tenderness, sometimes worse in the right lower quadrant, and, depending on the severity and extent, may disclose complications such as enterocutaneous fistulas and extraintestinal manifestations such as uveitis. Severe Crohn disease can result in intestinal obstruction from stricture or inflammation, bowel perforation, intraabdominal abscess, and the formation of additional fistulas. Diagnosis occurs through imaging and biopsy. Laboratory analyses often show increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein. Fluoroscopic small bowel follow through commonly shows narrowing of the intestinal lumen at the terminal ileum, along with other additional sites of stricture or fistulous formation. Treatment depends upon the severity and extent of disease but often includes 5-aminosalicylic acid derivatives, topical or oral steroids, and biologic agents such as anti-TNF- inhibitors. Surgery is sometimes required.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, F, and G.</p><p>Diverticulitis (Choice B) refers to inflammation of one or more diverticula in the colon. It is more common in older individuals with chronic constipation and may present with lower quadrant abdominal tenderness or focal peritonitis. The presence of a fistula and chronic diarrhea is not suggestive of diverticulitis.</p><p>enterocutaneous fistulas and extraintestinal manifestations such as uveitis. severe Crohn disease can result in intestinal oDstruction trom stricture or inflammation, bowel perforation, intraabdominal abscess, and the formation of additional fistulas. Diagnosis occurs through imaging and biopsy. Laboratory analyses often show increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein. Fluoroscopic small bowel follow through commonly shows narrowing of the intestinal lumen at the terminal ileum, along with other additional sites of stricture or fistulous formation. Treatment depends upon the severity and extent of disease but often includes 5-aminosalicylic acid derivatives, topical or oral steroids, and biologic agents such as anti-TNF- inhibitors. Surgery is sometimes required.</p><p>Endometriosis (Choice C) refers to the presence of endometrial tissue outside of the uterus and can result in chronic abdominal or pelvic pain, but it does not cause fistulas and chronic diarrhea or weight loss.</p><p>Gastritis (Choice D) can lead to chronic upper gastrointestinal bleeding resulting in a positive fecal occult blood test, but the pain is usually epigastric and not in the lower quadrants.</p><p>Irritable bowel syndrome (Choice E) may present with abdominal pain that improves with defecation in addition to diarrhea with mucus, but patients typically exhibit a normal BMI and do not have occult gastrointestinal bleeding, Ischemic colitis (Choice F) is more common in older patients with cardiovascular or peripheral vascular disease and usually presents with abdominal pain following a meal or in the setting of thrombosis/embolism. It may also present with hematochezia, but fistulas and chronic diarrhea are not a feature of this illness.</p><p>Ulcerative colitis (Choice G) is the other variety of inflammatory bowel disease that only affects the colon and often presents with abdominal pain, hematochezia, and diarrhea associated with weight loss. Patients may also have extraintestinal manifestations such as uveitis, but the presence of an anal fistula and symptoms that are related to small bowel inflammation strongly argue in favor of Crohn disease.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: JPS is an autosomal dominant syndrome with incomplete penetrance that leads to the formation of polyps. Histologically, these are hamartomas that demonstrate dilated, mucus filled glands with inflammatory infiltrates in the lamina propria. The most common presenting symptom is hematochezia or occult blood loss in the first two decades of life.</p><p>Juvenile polyposis (JPS) is the most likely diagnosis in this pediatric patient with painless and intermittent hematochezia and polyps demonstrating dilated, cystic, mucus-filled glands. JPS is a genetic condition with incomplete penetrance that is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion and occurs as the result of mutations in the SMAD4 or BMPR1A genes. Patients typically present within the first two decades of life with painless rectal bleeding secondary to polyps, although other more severe manifestations include intussusception and malabsorptive syndromes. Colonoscopy often discloses numerous polyps, but the histology is characteristic and sets JPS apart from many other polyposis syndromes. Polyps in JPS may be of many different varieties, but the most common type are called hamartomas. Hamartomatous polyps are made up of various tissues within the gastrointestinal tract, and histologically, they have inflammatory cells within the lamina propria in addition to mucin-filled, dilated glands. Other associated findings include telangiectasias of the skin, the gastrointestinal tract, the liver, and the lungs. Iron deficiency anemia may develop in patients with chronic gastrointestinal bleeding and should be monitored. Screening for colorectal cancer should be performed every 1 to 3 years after diagnosis, and in some cases, patients may require a colectomy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F.</p><p>Cowden disease (Choice A) is a genetic syndrome, often, although not always, associated with a mutation in the PTEN tumor suppressor gene. Patients present with mucocutaneous findings including trichilemmomas, breast cancer, thyroid disease, endometrial or renal cell cancer, and a variety of gastrointestinal disorders including colonic, duodenal, and gastric polyps or colorectal cancer.</p><p>Crantita-Canada oundeama [Chaica RV ie chorartarizad hi slanaria anuehaduetranhy and anhme Tha nahine tand ta he hamaramatane and eantain ancinanhile Thie natiant dase</p><p>ly diagnosis in this pediatric patient with painless and intermittent hematochezia and polyps demonstrating e glands. JPS is a genetic condition with incomplete penetrance that is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion and occurs as the result of mutations in the SMAD4 or BMPR1A genes. Patients typically present within the first two decades of life with painless rectal bleeding secondary to polyps, although other more severe manifestations include intussusception and malabsorptive syndromes. Colonoscopy often discloses numerous polyps, but the histology is characteristic and sets JPS apart from many other polyposis syndromes. Polyps in JPS may be of many different varieties, but the most common type are called hamartomas. Hamartomatous polyps are made up of various tissues within the gastrointestinal tract, and histologically, they have inflammatory cells within the lamina propria in addition to mucin-filled, dilated glands. Other associated findings include telangiectasias of the skin, the gastrointestinal tract, the liver, and the lungs. Iron deficiency anemia may develop in patients with chronic gastrointestinal bleeding and should be monitored. Screening for colorectal cancer should be performed every 1 to 3 years after diagnosis, and in some cases, patients may require a colectomy.</p><p>Cronkite-Canada syndrome (Choice B) is characterized by alopecia, onychodystrophy, and polyps. The polyps tend to be hamartomatous and contain eosinophils. This patient does not demonstrate features consistent with this syndrome.</p><p>Familial adenomatous polyposis (Choice C) is an autosomal dominant genetic syndrome resulting from biallelic loss of function of the APC gene. It is characterized by the presence of thousands of adenomatous polyps arising after puberty.</p><p>Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (Choice D) is also known as Lynch syndrome. It is an autosomal dominant condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair genes that result in a higher risk of colon, endometrial, and ovarian cancer. Patients may develop adenomatous polyps, often with high-grade dysplasia.</p><p>Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (Choice F) is a genetic condition characterized by the triad of colonic polyps, mucocutaneous hyperpigmented macules, and an increased risk of gastrointestinal and non-gastrointestinal malignancy. This patient's presentation is not consistent with this diagnosis.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with UC, especially those with a history of extensive colitis, are at a higher risk for CRC. All patients with UC should undergo screening colonoscopy 8 years after the date of their diagnosis, with subsequent intervals determined by the findings on colonoscopy. Virtual colonoscopy is not a valid alternative for the evaluation of patients with a higher risk of CRC.</p><p>Colonoscopy now is the most appropriate cancer screening option for this patient. Colonoscopy guidelines differ based upon whether patients have an underlying genetic disorder that predisposes them to premature colorectal cancer (CRC) such as Lynch syndrome, a history of inflammatory bowel disease, or a family history of CRC. The risk for colon cancer in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC) increases with the duration, extent, and activity of disease. Patients with extensive colitis, a history of uncontrolled inflammation, or a disease duration of greater than 10 years are at the highest risk for CRC. Those with proctitis, left sided colitis, and a history of well-controlled disease are at a lower risk of CRC. Regardless, all patients should undergo screening colonoscopy 8 years from the date of their diagnosis as the risk of CRC increases dramatically after this time. The subsequent screening regimen is usually at 1- to 3-year intervals and is determined by findings at each colonoscopy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Colonoscopy at the age of 45 years (Choice B) or the age of 50 years (Choice C) are both incorrect. The United States Preventive Services Task Force previously recommended starting routine colonoscopy screening at age 50 years for all individuals at average risk, or between ages 40 years and 45 years for individuals at higher risk. However, guidelines have recently been updated and recommend consideration for routine screening colonoscopy starting at age 40 years for average risk individuals.</p><p>Virtual colonoscopy now (Choice D), at the age of 45 years (Choice E), or at the age of 50 years (Choice F) are all incorrect. Virtual colonoscopy, also known at CT colonography, involves placing a rectal tube to distend the colon with gas prior to obtaining CT images of the entire colon. This is a reasonable, although not ideal, alternative screening procedure for patients at average risk of CRC, but is not used for the evaluation of patients with a higher risk of CRC.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Barrett esophagus develops in individuals with chronic GERD and is histologically characterized by intestinal metaplasia. Over time, dysplasia can develop, predisposing a patient to esophageal adenocarcinoma. Patients with dysplasia on biopsy require a follow-up EGD in 6 months.</p><p>Repeat the EGD and biopsy in 6 months is the most appropriate next step in management. Barret esophagus refers to intestinal metaplasia of squamous epithelium in the esophagus and is usually a consequence of prolonged gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), which occurs when acidic gastric contents reflux backward through the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) into the esophagus. The mucosa of the esophagus is comprised of squamous epithelium and does not traditionally encounter such an acidic environment. Constant exposure to acidic intraluminal contents induces a change in cell type from squamous epithelium to the columnar glandular epithelium found in the intestines. as an adaptive response. Metaplasia can eventually lead to dysplasia, which is premalignant. Patients with confirmed Barrett esophagus should be evaluated at regular intervals determined by the presence and/or grade of dysplasia. This patient who has undergone Nissen fundoplication still requires follow up EGD in 6 months to assess for the presence of dysplasia. If dysplasia is still present, treatment involves ablation of the dysplastic cells via endoscopy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Continue proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy alone (Choice A) is incorrect. The prior presence of esophageal dysplasia still requires follow-up EGD. Repeat the EGD in the patient's symptoms recur (Choice B) is incorrect. The presence of dysplasia on this patient's prior EGD necessitates a repeat EGD regardless of symptoms.</p><p>Perform laser ablation of Barrett esophagus (Choice D) may be necessary if repeat EGD demonstrates dysplasia, but repeat EGD with biopsy is the initial requisite step.</p><p>Continue proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy alone (Choice A) is incorrect. The prior presence of esophageal dysplasia still requires follow-up EGD.</p><p>Repeat the EGD in the patient's symptoms recur (Choice B) is incorrect. The presence of dysplasia on this patient's prior EGD necessitates a repeat EGD regardless of symptoms. Perform laser ablation of Barrett esophagus (Choice D) may be necessary if repeat EGD demonstrates dysplasia, but repeat EGD with biopsy is the initial requisite step.</p><p>No further treatment is indicated (Choice E) is incorrect. This patient previously had dysplasia and requires follow-up EGD.</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Gastrointestinal hemorrhage can be a life-threatening emergency. The preferred management approach is to initially perform a colonoscopy, as lesions can be identified and treated at the same time. Radiolabeled erythrocyte scan and angiography can be used if no active site is identified on colonoscopy, or the patient is too unstable for the procedure. If endoscopic and angiographic therapies are unsuccessful at stopping the bleeding, surgical intervention is indicated. For known active sites of bleeding, a segmental colectomy is appropriate. If no specific site is identified, total colectamy is indicated.</p><p>Endoscopic management has not been successful at achieving hemostasis, and the patient continues to have a high rate of hemorrhage given the hemoglobin concentration did not change with the transfusion of 3 units of packed red blood cells. The radiolabeled erythrocyte scan has localized the bleed to the right lower quadrant, but angiography has not revealed the exact site. Without a known site, angiographic interventions (such as arterial embolization) cannot be performed. Given the rate of hemorrhage, the next step is surgical management. Though the bleed is localized to the right lower quadrant, segmental colectomy is associated with high rates of rebleeding and mortality in the absence of an identified lesion. The next appropriate step in management is total abdominal colectomy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Capsule endoscopy (Choice A) is more appropriate for stable, chronic gastrointestinal bleeding. If the site of bleeding is not evident from endoscopic work-up, lesions in the mid- distal small bowel can be evaluated using capsule endoscopy.</p><p>Left hemicolectomy (Choice B) would be indicated if a lesion were identified in the left colon by colonoscopy or angiography, and interventions via those routes were unsuccessful. For this patient, the right hemicolon is implicated, but no specific site has been identified.</p><p>Repeat angiography (Choice C) could be considered if the patient was stable; however, this patient continues to have low hemoglobin concentration despite aggressive blood product resuscitation. Angiography is specific but not sensitive, as it can only detect active bleeding at rates greater than 0.5 ml/min. For this patient, definitive control of hemorrhage via surgery should not be delayed.</p><p>Repeat radiolabeled erythrocyte scan (Choice D) is unlikely to provide further useful information at this time. It is more sensitive than angiography, as it can detect active bleeding at rates between 0.1 to 0.5 ml/min. However, it can only localize bleeding to general segments of bowel. Follow-up to a positive radiolabeled erythrocyte scan is colonoscopy, angiography, or surgery to isolate a lesion and contro! bleeding.</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pharyngoesophageal (Zenker) diverticulum occurs between the cricopharyngeus and inferior pharyngeal constrictor muscles as a result of incoordination of contraction between the two muscles. This results in increased pressure generation and subsequent diverticula formation in the weak connective tissue between the two muscles. Patients classically present with halitosis and progressive dysphagia.</p><p>The most appropriate next step in diagnosis is performing a barium swallow. Progressive dysphagia requires a prompt diagnostic workup. Dysphagia is caused by structural/anatomic lesions and/or neuromuscular/motility problems. Most patients will undergo esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) initially; however, patients with a history suggesting a lesion that is at risk for esophageal perforation should be evaluated with a barium swallow first. This includes patients with known or suspected laryngeal or esophageal cancer, history of caustic injury or radiotherapy to the esophagus, or clinical features suggestive of pharyngoesophageal (Zenker) diverticulum. Pharyngoesophageal (Zenker) diverticulum occurs within the hypopharynx between the cricopharyngeus muscle and inferior pharyngeal constrictor muscle. The mechanism of formation includes an uncoordinated swallowing mechanism, which results in muscle spasms of the cricopharyngeus leading to pulsion diverticula as the wall of the hypopharynx and superior esophagus weakens. Symptoms include halitosis, regurgitation of undigested food, and progressive dysphagia. Barium swallow shows a posterior, midline outpouching above or at the level of the cricopharyngeus. Treatment includes surgical repair and resection (diverticulectomy), as well as dietary modifications to include soft or liquid foods.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>24-Hour esophageal pH monitoring (Choice A) can be used to confirm the diagnosis of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and to monitor response to treatment. GERD typically presents with burning epigastric and lower chest pain that worsens with large meals, trigger foods, and supine positioning. Dysphagia as a symptom of GERD does occur; however, diagnostic evaluation for structural and motility abnormalities should be performed first.</p><p>Esophageal manometry (Choice B) is used to evaluate for esophageal motility disorders. It is typically performed after an EGD and barium swallow if the etiology of dysphagia is still uncertain.</p><p>Ultrasonography of the abdomen (Choice D) can be used to evaluate for bowel obstruction or ileus. It does not provide useful information in the workup of oropharyngeal or esophageal dysphagia.</p><p>Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (Choice E) is not recommended as the initial study in a patient with clinical features of pharyngoesophageal (Zenker) diverticulum, as blind endoscopy could result in life-threatening esophageal perforation.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients presenting with nonspecific abdominal complaints and any evidence of gastrointestinal bleeding (melena, hematochezia, hematemesis, positive occult blood in stool, unexplained iron deficiency anemia) should be initially evaluated with an EGD. This study allows for visualization of luminal lesions, acquisition of biopsy samples, and therapeutic interventions.</p><p>The most appropriate next step in management is esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD). Mild epigastric pain and nausea after eating is commonly caused by gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD); however, this patient also has occult blood in the stool. Evidence of a gastrointestinal bleed (melena, hematochezia, hematemesis, positive occult blood in stool, unexplained iron deficiency anemia) with these symptoms is an indication for EGD to rule out malignancy. Unexplained weight loss, anorexia, progressive dysphagia, and a first-degree relative with cancer are additional red flags. EGD can rule out additional diagnoses, such as esophagitis, and collect biopsy samples at the time of the procedure. Biopsy samples may look for Helicobacter pylori infection, malignancy, or autoimmune disorders.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Recommend avoidance of caffeine (Choice A), and other dietary changes, would be appropriate following a diagnosis of gastritis or GERD. However, the patient needs further workup to identify the etiology of his symptoms.</p><p>Serum Helicobacter pylori antibody assay (Choice B) is not commonly used, as serology cannot distinguish between an active or past infection. Urea breath testing or stool antigen testing may be useful if the initial EGD and biopsy are nondiagnostic.</p><p>Upper gastrointestinal series (Choice C) should be performed prior to an EGD for patients at risk of esophageal perforation. This includes patients with known or suspected laryngeal or esophageal cancer, history of caustic injury or radiotherapy to the esophagus, or clinical features suggestive of Zenker diverticulum. This patient does not have any concerning history, and EGD should be the initial diagnostic test.</p><p>CT scan of the abdomen (Choice D) may be a useful follow-up study if the EGD is inconclusive. However, cross-sectional imaging studies are less sensitive for lesions along the luminal surface. EGD is preferred for the initial workup as biopsy acquisition and any indicated therapeutic measures can be performed at the time of the study.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Toxic megacolon classically presents with high fever, tachycardia, hypotension, leukocytosis, enlarged and dilated bowel, and impaired colonic motility. Patients with conditions that result in colonic inflammation, such as ulcerative colitis, Crohn disease, infections (such as Clostridium difficile, enteric gram-negative bacteria, cytomegalovirus, Entamoeba histolytica), and ischemic injury, are at risk for developing toxic megacolon.</p><p>This patient's presentation is highly concerning for toxic megacolon, a rare but known complication of ulcerative colitis. Toxic megacolon is a life-threatening medical emergency caused by extensive inflammation of the colon. The classic constellation of clinical manifestations includes high fever, tachycardia, hypotension, leukocytosis, enlarged and dilated bowel, and impaired colonic motility. More generally, ulcerative colitis is a chronic inflammatory condition within the spectrum of inflammatory bowel disease that results in inflammation and ulcers of the colonic mucosa, typically beginning with the rectum and advancing proximally. Symptoms include chronic abdominal pain, weight loss, bloody diarrhea, abdominal bloating, and tenesmus. Extraintestinal manifestations such as uveitis also occur. In addition, because of the chronic inflammation of the colon, patients are at increased risk of developing colorectal carcinoma. Treatment of ulcerative colitis flares includes supportive care, immunomodulators, biologics, and steroids. In severe cases, a partial or total colectomy may be required, especially in cases of toxic megacolon.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Carcinoma of the colon with obstruction (Choice A) is another potential complication of chronic inflammatory bowel disease but is less likely to explain the patient's current presentation. Large bowel obstruction typically presents with abdominal pain, distension, obstipation, and constipation. X-rays typically show proximal colonic dilation with a transition point at the site of the obstruction.</p><p>Dysplasia of the colon with hemorrhage (Choice B), also called angiodysplasia, is a common cause of gastrointestinal bleeding and could explain the patient's hematochezia. However, the patient's other symptoms are more suggestive of toxic megacolon.</p><p>Enterocolonic fistula (Choice C) is uncommon in patients with ulcerative colitis. Fistula formation is more common in Crohn disease as the inflammatory process involves all layers of the bowel wall, while in ulcerative colitis inflammation is limited to the mucosa and submucosa.</p><p>Gastroenteritis (Choice D) is common and presents with a variety of clinical manifestations, including fever, nausea, abdominal pain, distention, and diarrhea. However, in a patient with known inflammatory bowel disease and colonic distension on imaging, more aggressive management for toxic megacolon is needed.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Ischemic colitis refers to insufficient blood supply to an area of the bowel and should be suspected in a patient with acute abdominal pain and hematochezia. Recent endovascular surgery, particularly aortic surgery, is a risk factor, as well as older age, coronary artery disease, hypercoagulability, and peripheral arterial disease.</p><p>Ischemic colitis refers to insufficient blood flow to an area of the intestine and should be suspected in a patient with abdominal pain and hematochezia. It can be caused by an occlusion of the mesenteric vasculature, such as from thromboembolism or dissection, or from hypotension or limited blood flow causing a watershed infarction. Aortic surgery is a known risk factor for ischemic colitis, often because of dislodgement of endovascular plaques with subsequent embolization, or because of hypotension. Risk factors for acute ischemic colitis include older age, coronary artery disease, hypercoagulability, peripheral arterial disease, or recent endovascular surgery. Typical symptoms of patients with acute ischemic colitis include crampy abdominal pain, tenderness over the affected bowel, and hematochezia. Ischemic colitis can be complicated by necrosis, viscus perforation, or ileus. Treatment typically involves supportive care, anticoagulation, antibiotics, and surgery in cases of bowel infarction, necrosis, or perforation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Aortoenteric fistula (Choice A) is an abnormal communication between the aorta and gastrointestinal lumen commonly caused by compression or inflammation of the aorta or bowel in proximity to each other, often in the setting of an abdominal aortic aneurysm. Patients may present with gastrointestinal bleeding, which may be mild or may lead to rapid exsanguination. This patient's history of recent surgery complicated by hypotension is more suggestive of ischemic colitis.</p><p>Cecal angiodysplasia (Choice B) refers to an abnormal dilation and ectasia of a cecal blood vessel. Cecal angiodysplasia is often acquired and presents in older adults with gastrointestinal bleeding. It would not be expected to cause acute gastrointestinal bleeding shortly following aortic surgery.</p><p>Diverticulosis (Choice C) is characterized by sac-like protrusions of the colonic wail and is the most common cause of painless hematochezia. Risk factors for the development of diverticulosis include abnormal colonic motility, high dietary intake of red meat and low dietary fiber, physical inactivity, and obesity. This patient's abdominal cramps and tenderness are not suggestive of diverticulosis.</p><p>Pseudomembranous colitis (Choice E) occurs in infection with Clostridium difficile, a bacterium that causes colitis in patients with recent exposure to antibiotics. Patients do not typically present with hematochezia.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Sigmoid volvulus, a rotation of the large bowel around its mesentery, presents with acute abdominal pain and distention, constipation, and vomiting, typically in elderly, debilitated patients. It causes a large bowel, luminal obstruction. X-ray typically shows a dilated, air-filled sigmoid colon, while CT scan may show a whirl of sigmoid colon around its mesenteric attachment.</p><p>Sigmoid volvulus is an uncommon cause of bowel obstruction and presents with acute onset abdominal pain and distention, constipation, and vomiting. Patients are typically elderly. If untreated, volvulus may lead to bowel necrosis or perforation secondary to bowel distention, which in tum may cause sepsis and septic shock. Sigmoid volvulus occurs when the sigmoid colon rotates around its mesentery, resulting in intestinal closed-loop luminal obstruction and vascular congestion. X-ray typically demonstrates a dilated, air-filled sigmoid colon, while CT scan may demonstrate a whirl of sigmoid colon around its mesenteric attachment. Patients without evidence of sepsis or bowel necrosis may be treated with flexible sigmoidoscopy, which reduces the affected segment of bowel back to normal anatomic position. Recurrent volvulus may require colopexy. Patients with evidence of sepsis or bowel necrosis require immediate surgical intervention.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Carcinoma of the colon (Choice A) may lead to colonic obstruction in the setting of a large or advanced colonic lesion. Patients may present with constipation, diarrhea, iron deficiency anemia, and weight loss.</p><p>Diverticulitis of the colon (Choice B) presents with left lower quadrant abdominal pain, sometimes accompanied by diarrhea, fever, and tenderness to palpation in the left lower quadrant. Diverticulitis does not typically lead to colonic obstruction in the acute setting, though may cause chronic stricture formation. lleocolic intussusception (Choice C) may occur and lead to colonic obstruction but occurs far more frequently in children than in adults. X-ray may also demonstrate an elongated soft tissue mass. In adults, intussusception is commonly associated with colorectal carcinoma.</p><p>Ischemic colitis (Choice D) may result from thromboembolic occlusion or from watershed infarction. Patients typically present with hematochezia and abdominal pain, and do not typically present with bowel obstruction. X-ray imaging may show bowel wall thickening or perforation.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hemorrhoids occur because of enlargement and distal displacement of the anal cushions, which are normal prominences of the anal mucosa composed of smooth muscle, vascular, and connective tissues, and play an important role in maintaining continence. Symptoms include pain with defecation, incontinence, hematochezia, and anal itching. Hemorrhoids may also undergo acute thrombosis and cause acute, often severe pain. The diagnosis is clinical and may be established with a rectal exam, which can be facilitated via anoscopy.</p><p>Hemorrhoids occur because of enlargement and distal displacement of the anal cushions, which are normal prominences of the anal mucosa composed of smooth muscle, vascular, and connective tissues, and play an important role in maintaining continence. Hemorrhoids may primarily involve tissue derived from the internal, superior hemorrhoidal plexus or the external, inferior hemorrhoidal plexus. Symptoms include pain with defecation, incontinence, hematochezia, and anal itching. Hemorrhoids may also undergo acute thrombosis and cause acute, often severe pain. The diagnosis is clinical and may be established with a rectal examination, which can be facilitated via anoscopy. Treatment is mainly supportive and includes a high fiber diet, sitz baths, and oral nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Band ligation, sclerotherapy, or surgical excision may be used for severe hemorrhoids or those that fail to improve with conservative management.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Barium enema (Choice A) is useful for the diagnosis of colonic polyps, colorectal carcinoma, and diverticulosis, but is not the best step in the evaluation of hemorrhoids.</p><p>Endorectal ultrasonography (Choice B) is useful for the evaluation of the prostate gland and to assess the depth of mucosal invasion of colorectal carcinoma. It plays no role in the evaluation of hemorrhoids.</p><p>CT scan of the abdomen (Choice C) is useful for the evaluation of diverticulitis, among other colorectal pathologies, which presents with left lower quadrant abdominal pain, sometimes accompanied by diarrhea, fever, and tenderness to palpation in the left lower quadrant. Diverticulitis does not produce palpable, tender masses at the anal margin. Colonoscopy (Choice E) is the modality of choice for the diagnosis of colorectal carcinoma. Colorectal carcinoma is among the most diagnosed cancers but is uncommon in</p><p>Colonoscopy (Choice E) is the modality of choice for the diagnosis of colorectal carcinoma. Colorectal carcinoma is among the most diagnosed cancers but is uncommon in younger patients and typically does not produce palpable, tender masses at the anal margin.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Choledocholithiasis, or the presence of a gallstone located within the bile duct, can result in acute cholangitis. Acute cholangitis commonly presents with fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice (Charcot triad), along with possible shock and altered mental status (Reynold pentad).</p><p>Cholelithiasis refers to the presence of a gallstone or gallstones in the gallbladder, and choledocholithiasis refers to the presence of a gallstone located within the biliary tree. Gallstones can result in varying degrees of obstruction, which dictates the severity of presenting symptoms. Cholelithiasis can result in colicky right upper quadrant or epigastric pain (biliary colic), nausea, or vomiting; with obstructive choledocholithiasis, increased direct bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase may be present on laboratory assay, along with persistent, non-colicky discomfort on examination. Hyperbilirubinemia can lead to scleral icterus and jaundice. Depending on the location of the bile duct stone, choledocholithiasis can result in acute pancreatitis, which manifests with epigastric pain and increased pancreatic enzymes (amylase, lipase). Acute cholangitis occurs secondary to acute bacterial infection of the biliary tree, typically in the setting of biliary instrumentation or choledocholithiasis as a precursor. Cholangitis describes a bacterial infection or inflammation of the biliary tree, and commonly presents with fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice (Charcot triad), along with possible shock and altered mental status (Reynold pentad). Treatment includes fluid resuscitation, antibiotic therapy, and procedural intervention to clear any obstruction and permit drainage of infection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Cholecystitis (Choice B) generally results from obstruction of the cystic duct by a gallstone with resultant bacterial infection of the gallbladder, and presents with right upper quadrant pain, nausea, and vomiting. Since it does not cause obstruction of the common bile duct, it would not typically present with jaundice. Gastritis (Choice C) classically presents with left upper quadrant and epigastric pain, nausea, and vomiting. Gastritis commonly occurs from alcohol abuse, excess use of non-</p><p>Gastritis (Choice C) classically presents with left upper quadrant and epigastric pain, nausea, and vomiting. Gastritis commonly occurs from alcohol abuse, excess use of non- steroidal anti-inflammatory medication, and smoking. It does not typically cause fever and would not result in jaundice.</p><p>Hepatitis (Choice D) from viral infections can present with right upper quadrant discomfort, fever, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, increased transaminases, and abnormal serologic studies (eg, HBsAg, Anti-HBs, Anti-HBc, Anti- HCV) showing titers indicative of the infection. However, this patient's 2 weeks of colicky pain after eating prior to the acute presentation is more indicative of a gallstone-related pathology.</p><p>Pancreatitis (Choice E) can also be caused by gallstones obstructing the ampulla of Vater, resulting in epigastric pain, and increased pancreatic enzymes. However, pancreatitis in absence of obstructive biliary ductal disease would not cause jaundice.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Esophageal perforation can be a complication of endoscopy leading to mediastinitis and pleural effusions. Diagnosis of a perforated esophagus is accomplished through water-soluble contrast esophagography.</p><p>Upper endoscopy is a generally safe procedure, though complications can include bleeding, aspiration, and perforation; depending on the purpose of the endoscopy, specific complications such as pancreatitis following endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) can occur. In the case of dysphagia in this patient, the endoscopy was performed and dilated an esophageal stricture to promote passage of food bolus. Stretching of smooth muscle, stricture, and connective tissue, especially if chronic inflammation has resulted in compromise of the stromal strength of the esophageal wall, can lead to perforation. A perforated esophagus is diagnosed on water-soluble contrast esophagography. An esophageal perforation permits translocation of gastrointestinal contents such as food, stomach acid, and bacteria into the mediastinum, which can lead to fatal mediastinitis if untreated. Mediastinitis can present with chest pain and a generally toxic appearance (eg, fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, diaphoresis). Management of esophageal perforation is through antibiotics and emergent surgical intervention.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>CT scan of the chest (Choice B) can diagnose esophageal perforation if esophageal contrast studies are negative and clinical suspicion remains high. It can also identify intrathoracic fluid collections that may require drainage. However, water-soluble contrast esophagography is the best first step in diagnosis.</p><p>Repeat upper gastrointestinal endoscopy (Choice C) would not be appropriate, as it may exacerbate the mediastinitis due to air insufflated during endoscopy.</p><p>Thoracentesis (Choice D) or thoracostomy (Choice E) may be necessary if translocated gastric contents develop into an empyema; however, such a complication would not be evident for several days and may be avoided altogether with the use of antibiotics.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: After surgery, patients may have moderate to severe pain that should be adequately treated with a multimodal pain regimen such as non-opiate and opiate analgesia.</p><p>Incisional pain after an abdominal surgery can be moderate to severe and typically lasts for days to weeks. Oral administration of non-opiate medications may be helpful for pain control, but many patients may require opioid analgesics to control severe pain. Typically, the approach to adequately controlling pain is multimodal. While opioid medications can offer strong analgesia, opiates are central nervous system depressants and can lead to dependence, addiction, and death. Opiate intoxication causes sedation, bradycardia and hypotension, depressed respiratory drive, and constricted pupils. Opiates are commonly used for analgesia and act on receptors within the gastrointestinal tract, reducing gut motility and thus causing constipation. Adjunctive pain medications, such as acetaminophen, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory medications, gabapentin, and others, can help reduce the use of opiate medications and reduce the risk for its side effects, tolerance, and dependence. Short-term therapy following surgery with opiate compounds is indicated, though care should be taken to avoid prolonged use due to the risk of side effects and dependence.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, and F.</p><p>Oral aspirin (Choice A) is effective for mild to moderate pain relief but would not adequately address postoperative pain. It also can affect bleeding, clotting, and healing, so would not be the first choice for analgesia in a postoperative patient.</p><p>Diazepam (Choice B) is a benzodiazepine that may be useful for managing agitation, anxiety, muscle spasms, or seizures. It does not have pain-relieving effects that would be appropriate for treating postoperative pain.</p><p>Oral morphine sulfate (Choice C) and other opiate medications can be helpful in treating severe acute pain. However, multimodal pain regimens such as those containing arataminanhan and athar nan-nnista madiratinne ara cafar and radiira tha rick af aniata taloranra ar danandanra</p><p>Transcutaneous fentanyl (Choice E) is typically used for opiate-dependent patients for basal administration of pain medicine. This patient has acute pain after a recent surgery, and a fentanyl patch would be inappropriate.</p><p>Infiltration of the wound with bupivacaine (Choice F) would be helpful for immediate pain, but only lasts for several hours and would not be adequate to help treat postoperative pain that can last for days.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In congenital biliary atresia, the common bile duct is occluded, diminutive, or absent, and bile is unable to pass into the duodenum. Biliary atresia presents with progressively worsening neonatal jaundice caused by conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, pale stool, dark urine, poor weight gain, and, in untreated cases, cirrhosis, coagulopathy, and portal hypertension.</p><p>In congenital biliary atresia, the common bile duct is occluded, diminutive, or absent, and bile is unable to pass into the duodenum. This precludes the use of bile in the digestion of dietary fats and causes fat malabsorption. In contrast to benign causes of neonatal jaundice, biliary atresia presents with progressively worsening jaundice (conjugated hyperbilirubinemia), pale stools, dark urine, poor weight gain, and, in untreated cases, cirrhosis, coagulopathy, and portal hypertension. Classically, benign etiologies of neonatal jaundice will improve by the second to third week of life, whereas biliary atresia will persist and often worsen within the first 8 weeks of life. Diagnosis consists of history and physical examination, liver function testing, HIDA scan or cholangiography, with definitive diagnosis occurring during exploratory surgery with visualization of atretic bile ducts. Treatment is through hepatoportoenterostomy (Kasai procedure), which restores biliary flow, reduces jaundice, and helps salvage the native liver function. Liver transplant may be required if the procedure is unsuccessful.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Decreased bilirubin conjugation by hepatocytes (Choice A) would cause indirect bilirubinemia, as opposed to the direct bilirubinemia seen in this case. Gilbert syndrome is a hereditary cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia caused by diminished activity of uridine glucuronosyltransferase. Patients generally present with mild jaundice during periods of physiologic stress. In most cases, the condition is harmless.</p><p>Decreased bilirubin excretion by hepatocytes (Choice B) occurs in Dubin-Johnson syndrome and Rotor syndrome, which are characterized by defective hepatocellular excretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile canaliculi. This results in a direct hyperbilirubinemia.</p><p>Decreased bilirubin uptake by hepatocytes (Choice C) occurs to a mild extent in Gilbert syndrome, which is characterized by transient episodes of mild jaundice in response to fasting and stress.</p><p>Increased bilirubin production (Choice E) can occur with hemolytic anemias, which result in indirect hyperbilirubinemia caused by the release of heme into the bloodstream during red blood cell lysis.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The fractional excretion of sodium in urine can help differentiate the cause of acute kidney injury. Prerenal and intrinsic kidney injury can be distinguished by the fractional excretion of sodium, which is &lt;1% in prerenal and &gt;2% in intrinsic kidney injury.</p><p>Acute kidney injury is defined as an acute increase in creatinine by greater than or equal to 0.3 mg/dL or 1.5 times above baseline creatinine, occasionally with oliguria or anuria. Acute kidney injury can be prerenal (because of dehydration, low vascular perfusion states such as congestive heart failure, or intrinsic such as acute tubular necrosis) or postrenal because of obstruction. A common cause is acute tubular necrosis, which can be caused by ischemia in the setting of prolonged hypoperfusion or toxins. A way to distinguish between the causes of acute kidney injury is by measuring the fractional excretion of sodium. The fractional excretion of a solute measures the percentage of the original filtered amount that is present in the excreted urine. The calculations are made using the plasma and urine concentrations of the sodium and creatinine. Prerenal and intrinsic kidney injury can be distinguished by the fractional excretion of sodium, which is &lt;1% in prerenal and &gt;2% in intrinsic kidney injury. In acute tubular necrosis, laboratory evaluation will show a blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio of less than 20, a fractional excretion of sodium greater than 2%, and muddy brown casts on urinalysis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Administration of spironolactone (Choice A) would not likely explain this presentation. Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that serves as an aldosterone receptor antagonist. It would not be appropriate to start a diuretic before diagnosing the cause of acute kidney injury, as it can exacerbate the kidney injury.</p><p>Measurement of plasma free hemoglobin concentration (Choice B), the amount of hemoglobin that is not enclosed in red blood cells, may help diagnose hemolytic anemia and explain renal tubular injury from free hemoglobin. Measurement of free hemoglobin concentration would not be informative in delineating causes of acute kidney injury in the setting of blood transfusion unless hemolysis or incompatibility were expected.</p><p>Renal ultrasonography (Choice D) is useful in evaluation of the patient's increased serum creatinine level and should be performed, but it is not likely to disclose the diagnosis on its own in a case of hypotension and blood loss preceding kidney injury.</p><p>Replacement of the urinary catheter (Choice E) would be appropriate if the acute kidney injury is caused by urinary obstruction. However, this typically would present with abdominal and suprapubic distention, or evidence of a retroperitoneal process in the history or examination.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Urethral injuries are characterized by difficulty voiding, blood at the urethral meatus, perineal ecchymosis, and may be associated with a pelvic fracture. Urethral injuries are evaluated by retrograde urethrography, which involves the injection of contrast into the urethra to evaluate for the extravasation of contrast, indicating urethral discontinuity.</p><p>A patient with pelvic injury and blood at the urethral meatus raises suspicion for a urethral injury. Urethral injuries are characterized by difficulty voiding, blood at the urethral meatus, perineal ecchymosis, and may be associated with a pelvic fracture. Straddle injuries can cause trauma to the anterior urethra, while pelvic fractures may injure the posterior urethra. Urethral injuries are evaluated by retrograde urethrography, which involves the injection of contrast into the urethra to evaluate for the extravasation of contrast, indicating urethral discontinuity. Depending on the location and severity of urethral injury, patients may require surgical repair or the placement of a urethral catheter to help promote healing and prevent complications of urinary extravasation, infection, urethrocutaneous fistula, or urethral stricture.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, and F.</p><p>Cystography (Choice A) is indicated for evaluation of the urinary bladder when a bladder injury is suspected. This allows for characterization of an intraperitoneal versus extraperitoneal bladder rupture, but it would not be the best first choice in suspected urethral injury.</p><p>Intravenous pyelography (Choice B) involves the intravenous injection of radiopaque contrast to subsequently visualize the kidney collecting system, ureters, and bladder under fluoroscopy. This allows for the investigation of suspected urinary tract anatomic abnormalities, malignancies, obstruction, malformation, and strictures.</p><p>Ultrasonography of the pelvis (Choice D) such as that performed in a focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST) examination would be able to identify free peritoneal fluid; similarly, a CT scan of the pelvis (Choice E) can identify solid organ injury and the presence of free fluid, but neither would be able to directly identify a urethral injury.</p><p>Insertion of a urinary catheter (Choice F) would be contraindicated as the first step in management in a patient with blood at the urethral meatus, portending a likely urethral injury. It may cause further trauma and injury to the urethra and internal pelvic structures without the injury first being delineated on retrograde urethrography.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Nipple discharge can be caused by lactation, physiologic mechanisms, or pathologic lesions. Lactation and physiologic mechanisms more commonly present with bilateral discharge, whereas pathologic lesions are more frequently associated with unilateral discharge. Intraductal papilloma, a benign lesion of the ductal lining, is the most common cause of unilateral bloody nipple discharge.</p><p>The most common cause of unilateral bloody nipple discharge is an intraductal papilloma, which may be associated with focal ductal carcinoma in situ. However, an intraductal papilloma itself is a benign lesion. Nipple discharge may be caused by lactation, physiologic mechanisms such as galactorrhea, or pathologic lesions such as breast cancer or infection. Evaluation includes a careful history and physical examination, with particular attention paid to whether the discharge is unilateral or bilateral, if it is from a single or multiple ducts, and whether it is associated with any breast abnormalities (eg, skin changes, breast lumps), Lactation and physiologic discharge are more commonly bilateral and from multiple ducts, whereas pathological lesions more commonly present unilaterally. Further diagnostic evaluation of pathologic discharge, as in this patient, often involves ultrasonography or mammography based on patient age. If an abnormality is detected, biopsy is recommended.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Ductal carcinoma in situ (Choice A) may present with unilateral bloody nipple discharge, as well as a breast mass, and it may be associated with an intraductal papilloma. However, this patient's mammography showed normal findings, making breast cancer such as ductal carcinoma in situ unlikely.</p><p>Fibroadenoma (Choice B) is a well-circumscribed, mobile breast mass generally seen in women of reproductive age. Mammography or ultrasonography would show a solid mass. Fibroadenomas do not routinely present with nipple discharge.</p><p>Paget disease of the breast (Choice D) describes skin changes of the nipple and areola that are often related to underlying malignancy. These skin changes include discoloration, erythema, and ulceration. While Paget disease of the breast can also present with bloody unilateral nipple discharge, this patient does not have the skin findings associated with Paget disease, nor does she have evidence of malignancy on mammography.</p><p>Prolactin-producing pituitary adenoma (Choice E) can cause hyperprolactinemia, which can cause galactorrhea. Galactorrhea presents with bilateral, multiductal discharge of clear or milky fluid in patients that are not physiologically lactating. Other symptoms in premenopausal women include menstrual cycle irregularities and infertility, as well as headache or visual changes from the adenoma itself. This patient has unilateral nipple discharge, which makes a pathologic cause, such as intraductal papilloma, more likely.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Breast lesions are common in women and may be benign or malignant. Initial diagnostic evaluation includes a thorough history and physical examination, as well as diagnostic imaging with ultrasonography and/or mammography depending on the patient's age. Cystic lesions should undergo fine needle aspiration, whereas solid lesions more commonly require core needle or excisional biopsies.</p><p>Breast masses are common in women. Evaluation of a new breast mass includes a thorough history and physical examination. Benign masses are commonly small, well- circumscribed, and mobile, whereas malignant lesions are more likely to be large, poorly defined, adherent to underlying tissue, and associated with skin or nipple changes. Further diagnostic evaluation of a breast mass depends on the patient's age, as well as the clinical concern for malignancy. In patients younger than 30 years old, ultrasonography is utilized as the initial imaging modality to further analyze a palpable breast mass. For older patients, mammography is the initial imaging technique of choice. Occasionally, abnormalities on one imaging modality are confirmed with the alternative imaging modality. In this case, the patient's mass visualized on mammography was confirmed with breast ultrasonography, which disclosed a cystic-appearing lesion. Cystic lesions should be carefully observed or undergo needle aspiration for cytologic evaluation or symptomatic benefit. A simple cyst that completely collapses after aspiration requires no further management.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Excisional biopsy (Choice A) involves surgical excision of the entire breast mass, whereas an incisional biopsy (Choice B) removes only a portion of the mass. These procedures may be utilized for solid lesions that have imaging findings or clinical symptoms concerning for malignancy. This patient has a cystic-appearing lesion more suitable for needle aspiration, a less invasive option than biopsy that may provide diagnostic as well as therapeutic benefit.</p><p>Mammography-localized core needle biopsy (Choice C) may be useful in evaluating a solid lesion that is not evident on physical examination. Mammography localization allows the biopsy to include tissue more reliably from the area of imaging abnormality. This patient's lesion is cystic, palpable on physical examination, and easily evident on ultrasonography, making fine needle aspiration the more appropriate next step in management.</p><p>MRI (Choice D) is rarely utilized in the evaluation of breast lesions. It is commonly used as a screening modality for patients at high risk for invasive breast carcinoma, such as those with the BRCA-1 and BRCA-2 genes, given its high sensitivity in detecting breast cancer. It may also be used preoperatively to evaluate the extent of disease in women with newly diagnosed breast cancer or to evaluate for occult breast malignancy in patients with axillary lymphadenopathy. This patient's lesion is visible on both ultrasonography and mammography, making MRI unnecessary.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Breast masses require a thorough evaluation to exclude malignancy, with a complete history and physical examination, imaging with ultrasonography and/or mammography, and biopsy if indicated. While many breast lesions are benign, some can be associated with an increased risk of breast cancer. For example, lobular carcinoma in situ increases a patient's risk for developing invasive breast carcinoma in the future, which may be ipsilateral or contralateral.</p><p>Breast masses are common in women, and breast cancer is the most common non-dermatologic malignancy in women. Evaluation of a new breast mass includes a thorough history and physical examination. Benign masses are commonly small, well-circumscribed, and mobile, whereas malignant lesions are more likely to be large, poorly defined, adherent to underlying tissue, and associated with skin or nipple changes. Further diagnostic evaluation of a breast mass depends on the patient's age, as well as the clinical concer for malignancy, and typically includes mammography and/or ultrasonography, in addition to biopsy when indicated. Although a significant portion of breast masses are benign, they may be associated with future malignancy. Lobular carcinoma in situ is a benign proliferative lesion with atypical cell characteristics that most commonly occurs in pre- menopausal women. However, it is associated with an increase in the risk of development of invasive breast carcinoma, which may occur in either the ipsilateral or contralateral breast, although it is not considered a precursor lesion. Therefore, this patient is at greatest risk for developing invasive ductal carcinoma in the next 10 years. Other risk factors for the development of breast cancer include a family history of premenopausal breast cancer, genetic mutation (eg BRCA 7 or 2), early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, obesity, exogenous estrogen use, and African American ethnicity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, 8, C, and E.</p><p>Bone metastasis (Choice A) is often associated with breast, prostate, and lung cancer. Typical presentations include bone pain, hypercalcemia (eg, abdominal pain, altered mental status, nephrolithiasis), and pathologic fractures. Given this patient's history of lobular carcinoma in situ, she is most at risk for the development of invasive breast cancer in the future, which may or may not be associated with metastasis.</p><p>Fibrocystic breast condition (Choice B) is a benign disease of the breasts characterized by multiple cystic masses that are often hormonally responsive and fluctuate in size. It may present with a palpable, painful breast mass that appears fluid-filled on imaging. Lobular carcinoma in situ is associated with the future development of invasive ductal carcinoma, not fibrocystic breast disease.</p><p>Intraductal papilloma (Choice C) is a benign growth of the lining of the breast duct. It most commonly presents with unilateral bloody nipple discharge. It is not associated with lobular carcinoma in situ.</p><p>Ovarian metastasis (Choice E) is often secondary to gastrointestinal cancer, such as colorectal cancer, or breast cancer, although the relative incidence of ovarian metastasis is low. They may be asymptomatic or present with abdominal pain and distention, with an adnexal mass on exam. Lobular carcinoma in situ is a benign lesion and would not cause ovarian metastasis.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Blunt trauma to the external genitalia in a male can lead to damage to the penis, scrotum, testes, and anterior urethra, including penile fracture, scrotal or testicular hematoma, testicular vascular injury, testicular rupture, and disruption of the bulbar urethra, among others. A disrupted blood supply to the testes is a surgical emergency and requires prompt surgical exploration to restore blood flow.</p><p>Blunt trauma of the external genitalia in a male can lead to injury to the testes, penis, and anterior urethra. Specifically, blunt trauma to the penis can result in penile fracture, whereas trauma to the scrotum can cause rupture of the testes, vascular injury, and testicular torsion and hematoma. A straddle injury, as in this patient, causes injury to the perineum with subsequent disruption of the bulbar urethra. Penile fractures result with pain, edema, and ecchymoses, whereas testicular rupture or vascular injury presents with testicular swelling and ecchymoses, with disruption of the testicular anatomy. Disruption of the bulbar urethra presents with hematuria on urinalysis. This patient has many genitourinary abnormalities following his straddle injury, including a possible penile fracture, scrotal hematoma, and possible testicular vascular injury and urethral injury. The absence of blood flow to the testes is a surgical emergency and requires prompt evaluation by surgical exploration to try to restore blood flow and prevent testicular necrosis and resultant infertility. The next most appropriate step in management of this patient is surgical exploration.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Renal ultrasonography (Choice A) may be used to evaluate the kidney if there was suspicion of upper genitourinary tract injury. This more commonly occurs following blunt or penetrating trauma to the abdomen or retroperitoneum or on rapid deceleration injuries. It is less common with a straddle injury and would be likely to present with hematuria, flank pain, and hemodynamic instability.</p><p>MRI of the pelvis (Choice B) may be useful to evaluate for other occult injuries following this patient's fall. However, given the absence of blood flow to his testes, the time required for an MRI of the pelvis would likely lead to permanent damage to or possible necrosis of the testes.</p><p>Intravenous pyelography (Choice C) may be used to evaluate the integrity of the genitourinary system. It is obtained by injecting intravenous contrast and then obtaining an abdominopelvic x-ray. It is rarely used in emergency medicine and traumatic injury evaluation, as the availability of CT scan has minimized its utility. This patient has absence of blood flow to his testes, necessitating emergent surgical exploration which should not be delayed by further imaging.</p><p>Cystoscopy (Choice D) can be used to evaluate the urethra, bladder, and ureters. However, it is relatively contraindicated in patients with evidence of a urethral injury as it can lead to further disruption of the injury. Thus, it would be an inappropriate next step in the management of this patient.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with toxic thyroid adenomas present with signs and symptoms of hyperthyroidism, while examination may disclose a dominant thyroid nodule. Differentiating between toxic adenomas and other causes of hyperthyroidism is usually straightforward and is aided by using thyroid ultrasonography, radioactive iodine uptake scan, and measurement of anti-thyroid antibodies. Treatment usually involves resection of the toxic nodule.</p><p>Toxic adenoma is the most likely diagnosis in this patient with increased serum thyroxine, low serum thyroid-stimulating hormone, and a palpable, single thyroid nodule on examination. Additionally, the patient has symptoms consistent with thyrotoxicosis, including irritability, weight loss, and diarrhea. A toxic adenoma presents as a solitary thyroid nodule accompanied by symptoms of hyperthyroidism such as heat intolerance, sweating, hair loss, warm, flushed skin, amenorrhea, diarrhea, palpitations, and weight loss. Vital signs may show hyperthermia, tachycardia, tachypnea, and hypertension. The adenoma autonomously produces triiodothyronine (T,) and thyroxine (T,), unregulated by the normal negative feedback mechanism. Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) will be low, as it remains governed by the levels of serum T, and T,. Definitive diagnosis requires evaluation with ultrasonography to identify the thyroid nodule and a radioactive iodine uptake scan, which will demonstrate a solitary hyperfunctioning (‘hot’) nodule. Treatment generally requires partial thyroidectomy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and |. Defect in thyroxine (T,) biosynthesis (Choice A) is incorrect, as this would lead to decreased levels of thyroxine, not increased levels. Graves disease (Choice B) is unlikely in this case. Patients with Graves disease have symptoms of thyrotoxicosis but usually demonstrate a diffusely enlarged thyroid on</p><p>Graves disease (Choice B) is unlikely in this case. Patients with Graves disease have symptoms of thyrotoxicosis but usually demonstrate a diffusely enlarged thyroid on examination, rather than a solitary nodule.</p><p>Multinodular goiter (Choice C) typically does not cause thyrotoxicosis, and the physical examination would disclose numerous thyroid nodules, not a single nodule.</p><p>Riedel thyroiditis (Choice D) is incorrect, as patients with this condition are most often euthyroid or hypothyroid. Physical examination usually discloses a diffusely enlarged, firm thyroid.</p><p>Thyroid carcinoma (Choice E) refers to one of several different types of thyroid cancer, including papillary and medullary thyroid carcinoma, among others. Thyroid malignancies typically do not secrete thyroxine.</p><p>Thyroid cyst (Choice F) describes a type of thyroid lesion that can occasionally be palpated or visualized on thyroid ultrasonography. Cysts are nonfunctioning and do not secrete thyroxine, thus they do not cause hyperthyroidism.</p><p>Thyroiditis (Choice G) refers to inflammation of the thyroid gland that can occur because of several conditions, including subacute thyroiditis, postpartum thyroiditis, and medication- induced thyroiditis. Patients may become transiently hyperthyroid, but these symptoms rarely last longer than 2 months before patients return to a euthyroid or hypothyroid state. This patient has demonstrated symptoms for 6 months.</p><p>Triiodothyronine (T) thyrotoxicosis (Choice |) is common in patients with Graves disease and results from the increased secretion of T, in addition to increased peripheral conversion of T, to T,, As well, this may occur in patients taking T3 supplements.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Parathyroid hormone concentrations are decreased in the setting of hypoparathyroidism, which results in subsequent hypocalcemia. This may be caused by the inadvertent removal of one or more parathyroid glands during a thyroidectomy. Symptoms of hypocalcemia include perioral paresthesia, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, prolonged QT interval, Chvostek sign, and Trousseau sign. Treatment is with intravenous calcium gluconate.</p><p>Intravenous calcium gluconate should be given to this patient with signs and symptoms of hypocalcemia following thyroidectomy. The parathyroid glands are located along the posterior aspect of the thyroid gland and, while not frequent, can sometimes be inadvertently removed during a thyroidectomy. The parathyroid glands secrete parathyroid hormone (PTH), which has numerous actions, including the stimulation of vitamin D activation in the kidneys and resorption of calcium from the bones to maintain calcium homeostasis. Loss of appropriate PTH production leads to hypocatcemia, with resultant symptoms that include perioral paresthesia, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, and prolonged QT interval. The Chvostek sign, in which tapping of the facial nerve leads to spasm of the facial muscles, and Trousseau sign, in which an inflated blood pressure cuff occluding the brachial artery causes carpopedal spasm, are two physical examination findings that may indicate hypocalcemia. Treatment is with intravenous calcium gluconate. For these reasons, calcium and PTH are often monitored immediately following total thyroidectomy to aid in the early detection and treatment of hypocalcemia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Intravenous magnesium sulfate (Choice B) is the treatment of choice for patients with symptomatic hypomagnesemia or QT prolongation with risk of dysrhythmia. Signs and symptoms of hypomagnesemia may include tremor, weakness, delirium, and arrhythmias. Hypocalcemia is frequently encountered in conjunction with hypomagnesemia, but this patient's recent surgery and current symptoms are more suggestive of hypocalcemia.</p><p>Oral calcium carbonate (Choice C) can be used for the treatment of chronic hypoparathyroidism with mild hypocalcemia, but this patient has symptoms of severe hypocalcemia that warrant treatment with intravenous calcium.</p><p>Oral magnesium oxide (Choice D) is sometimes used as an antacid or as treatment for chronic hypomagnesemia. Treatment with magnesium oxide would not be beneficial to this patient with symptoms of hypocalcemia.</p><p>Oral vitamin D0; (Choice E) is a dietary supplement used to treat patients with mild vitamin D deficiency. Patients with mild vitamin D deficiency usually have normal calcium levels, although profoundly low vitamin D levels can occasionally lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism. This patient's history suggests hypoparathyroidism secondary to surgical resection and vitamin D supplementation would be of little value at this time.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Antidiuretic hormone causes water reabsorption in the collecting duct of the renal tubules in response to increased plasma osmolality or decreased arterial blood volume. It can also be released independently of these factors in response to surgery and pain, cases of neurologic injury, as a medication side effect, or because of ectopic production by a malignant tumor or lung disease. Secretion of antidiuretic hormone often leads to concentrated oliguria with hyponatremia.</p><p>Antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin, ADH) is a hormone secreted by the posterior pituitary that leads to water reabsorption in the collecting duct of the renal tubule by increasing the amount of aquaporin channels. It also increases systemic vascular resistance by causing peripheral vasoconstriction and consequently increases blood pressure. Its secretion is stimulated by increased plasma osmolality and decreased arterial blood volume. The syndrome of inappropriate secretion of ADH (SIADH) occurs when ADH is secreted independently of plasma osmolality or arterial blood volume. This most commonly occurs in neurologic injury, as a medication side effect, or because of ectopic production by a malignant tumor or lung disease. However, surgery can also cause an increase in secretion of ADH, thought to be secondary to pain. Inappropriate secretion of ADH in relation to plasma osmolality and blood volume results in decreased urine output; when this occurs, the residual urine is concentrated, with urine studies showing a urine osmolality above 100 mOsmol/kg and urine sodium above 40 mEq/L. While hypovolemia or obstruction can cause oliguria, these would likely also increase the patient's serum urea nitrogen and creatinine. As these parameters are normal, this patient's oliguria is most likely secondary to an increased concentration of ADH.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Cortisol (Choice B) is increased in Cushing syndrome, which presents with hypertension, central adiposity, moon facies, hirsutism, striae, proximal muscle weakness, and insulin resistance. This can be caused by Cushing disease, exogenous glucocorticoids, or a variety of other etiologies. However, Cushing syndrome more commonly presents with polyuria than oliguria.</p><p>Epinephrine (Choice C) and norepinephrine (Choice E) excess would be expected to present with signs of sympathetic nervous system activation, such as tachycardia and hypertension, This consequently would lead to increased cardiac output and theoretically increased renal perfusion, which may be associated with increased rather than decreased urine output.</p><p>Glucagon (Choice D) is secreted by the pancreas in response to hypoglycemia. It results in glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and decreased fatty acid synthesis. While there are receptors for glucagon within the renal tubules, it is generally not considered to contribute significantly to urine volume.</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hyponatremia is defined as a serum sodium of less than 135 mEq/L and may be acute or chronic. Acute hyponatremia is more likely to present with severe symptoms, whereas chronic hyponatremia is more likely to be asymptomatic. Acute, severe hyponatremia can cause symptoms of obtundation, seizures, and respiratory arrest, and should rapidly be corrected with hypertonic saline. Chronic hyponatremia, however, should be corrected over several days to prevent central pontine myelinolysis.</p><p>Hyponatremia, or serum sodium values less than 135 mEq/L, can be acute or chronic and is caused by a variety of etiologies. Acute hyponatremia is generally considered hyponatremia that develops in less than 48 hours and is more commonly symptomatic, whereas chronic hyponatremia has been present for more than 48 hours and may be asymptomatic. Mild hyponatremia can present with confusion, nausea, and fatigue, whereas severe hyponatremia can cause obtundation, seizures, and respiratory arrest. The syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) is one common etiology of hyponatremia, caused by an increase in antidiuretic hormone (ADH) independent of plasma osmolality or arterial blood volume, often secondary to a neurologic insult as seen in this patient. ADH increases free water absorption in the collecting duct of the renal tubule, with subsequent free water intake and decreased urine output leading to hyponatremia. Acute, severe hyponatremia (especially when complicated by seizure activity) is life- threatening and should be treated with hypertonic saline (3% sodium chloride) to symptom resolution, with gradual correction to normal once the acute emergency has resolved. Chronic hyponatremia should be slowly corrected to prevent central pontine myelinolysis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Desmopressin (Choice A) is a synthetic analog of antidiuretic hormone or vasopressin. It increases water reabsorption in the collecting duct of the renal tubules and can thus worsen hyponatremia. Administering desmopressin would be an inappropriate next step in management of this patient.</p><p>Glucose (Choice B) is used to treat hypoglycemia, which can present with altered mental status, nausea, diaphoresis, and tremors or seizures. It also presents with tachycardia and tachypnea caused by sympathetic activation, which are not present in this patient. Administration of glucose additionally would not improve this patient's hyponatremia.</p><p>Naloxone (Choice C) is an opioid antagonist that is used to treat opioid overdose. Opioid overdose often presents with somnolence, bradypnea, and respiratory arrest in severe cases. Opioid overdose does not present with seizures, and therefore, naloxone would not be the appropriate treatment for this patient.</p><p>Pentobarbital (Choice D) is a barbiturate medication that may be used to treat seizures. However, this patient's seizures are secondary to her acute, severe hyponatremia and the underlying cause of the seizures should be treated. This would best be accomplished with 3% sodium chloride.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Severe hyperkalemia may present with peaked T-waves on ECG, as well as cardiac arrhythmia. The first step in management of hyperkalemia includes the administration of intravenous calcium to prevent cardiac cell excitability. Subsequent management includes medications that cause the transfer of potassium out of the serum and into the cells (eg, insulin, albuterol, sodium bicarbonate) along with agents that move potassium out of the body through the urine or stool,</p><p>Postoperative kidney injury is a risk of surgery, particularly in patients with pre-existing renal disease. This risk is further increased by surgeries that are associated with significant fluid shifts, such as intra-abdominal surgeries and cardiac surgeries, prolonged hypotension, and in elderly patients. This patient is demonstrating symptomatic hyperkalemia with peaked T-waves on ECG and increased creatinine and urea nitrogen, indicative of postoperative renal failure, likely secondary to acute tubular necrosis in the setting of intraoperative hypotension. Hyperkalemia can be a deadly consequence of renal failure, as it can lead to cardiac arrhythmias and arrest. The first step in management of hyperkalemia includes administration of intravenous calcium to prevent cardiac cell excitability. Subsequent management includes medications that cause the transfer of potassium out of the serum and into the cells (eg, insulin, albuterol, sodium bicarbonate) along with agents that move potassium out of the body through the urine (eg, loop diuretics) and stool (eg, exchange resins). Hemodialysis may be required in emergent and severe cases.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Repeat measurement of serum potassium concentration (Choice A) may be appropriate in some cases of hyperkalemia, such as that due to hemolysis of the specimen. However, this patient has symptomatic hyperkalemia with peaked T-waves on ECG. Thus, he should be treated urgently with calcium chloride to prevent cardiac dysrhythmias.</p><p>Low-potassium diet (Choice B) may be appropriate for patients with chronic hyperkalemia in the setting of chronic renal disease. However, a low-potassium diet would require a significant period to reliably reduce this patient's potassium to normal levels, during which time he may have complications of hyperkalemia, such as cardiac dysrhythmias.</p><p>Rectal sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate) therapy (Choice C) would not be an inappropriate choice in the management of this patient, as it takes hours to days to be effective and the patient would remain at risk for the complications of hyperkalemia during that time. Additionally, Kayexalate is associated with ischemic colitis and is being replaced by safer agents (eg, sodium zirconium sulfate) in practice.</p><p>Immediate hemodialysis (Choice E) is an appropriate treatment for severe metabolic acidosis, electrolyte disturbances such as hyperkalemia, certain intoxications, fluid overload, and uremia. However, prior to its initiation, less invasive measures should be attempted and may sufficiently avoid the need for dialysis. Additionally, the initiation of dialysis requires placement of a hemodialysis line and consultation with a nephrologist, time during which the patient may develop cardiac dysrhythmias.</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Postoperative hypotension may be caused by bleeding, under-resuscitation during the procedure, adverse effects of medication, and sepsis. Postoperative bleeding often presents with significant tachycardia that progresses to hypotension once the tachycardia is unable to compensate for the hypovolemia. Other signs of postoperative bleeding include a low central venous pressure and a decrease in hematocrit, as well as swelling or ecchymoses of the surgical site. Treatment includes fluid resuscitation, blood product transfusion, pressor therapy, and prompt surgical re-exploration.</p><p>Sudden postoperative hypotension is presumed to be caused by bleeding until determined otherwise. Other causes of postoperative hypotension include under-resuscitation during the procedure, medication side effects, and sepsis. Hypovolemia, whether due to anemia or fluid depletion, results in a compensatory tachycardia to try to maintain mean arterial pressure (MAP). However, with significant enough hypovolemia, the tachycardia is unable to maintain the MAP, the blood pressure subsequently falls, and the patient becomes hypotensive. Other evidence of postoperative bleeding in this patient includes a decrease in hematocrit from 42% to 28% (although a decrease in hematocrit may not always be present with an acute bleed), and a low central venous pressure indicating hypovolemia. Other signs of bleeding include pallor and swelling or ecchymoses of the surgical site. Management of postoperative hypotension caused by bleeding includes fluid resuscitation, blood product transfusion, pressor therapy, and prompt surgical re-exploration.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Addisonian crisis (Choice A) occurs in adrenal insufficiency. Adrenal insufficiency (depending on level — primary, secondary, tertiary) presents variably with weight loss, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, hyperpigmentation, hypotension, hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia. Patients with Addison disease may develop an Addisonian crisis in response to a variety of stressors, such as severe infection or a surgical procedure. This often presents with lethargy, confusion, and hypotension. This patient has no other symptoms of Addison disease but does have a decreased central venous pressure and hematocrit, making postoperative bleeding a more likely cause of this patient's hypotension.</p><p>Myocardial infarction (Choice B) classically presents with crushing substernal chest pain that radiates to the left shoulder and jaw, associated with shortness of breath, diaphoresis, and nausea. If the myocardial ischemia and infarction areas includes a significant portion of the myocardium, a decrease in cardiac output can occur with consequent hypotension. However, a myocardial infarction would be associated with a significantly higher concentration of CK-MB, which is an isoenzyme specific for myocardial damage.</p><p>Pulmonary embolus (Choice D) presents with tachycardia, tachypnea, hypoxia, shortness of breath, and pleuritic chest pain. If massive, it can lead to hypotension and cardiac arrest. When it occurs in post-surgical patients, it more commonly occurs several days after the procedure, often secondary to prolonged immobilization.</p><p>Septic shock (Choice E) generally presents with fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, altered mental status, and signs of end-organ damage, such as an acute kidney injury or transaminitis, in a patient with a known or suspected infection. While it can occur immediately after surgery when the surgery involves infected tissues, it more often occurs several days following a procedure. The decrease in hematocrit in this case is more closely associated with hemorrhagic shock.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Jehovah’s Witnesses are a Christian group of patients who have a religious objection to receiving blood products, including whole blood, packed red blood cells, platelets, and plasma. If informed consent is obtained and the patient is competent to make medical decisions, they have the right to refuse blood transfusion, even if it may be lifesaving. However, in the event of emergency, a patient with hemorrhagic shock (as evidenced by hemodynamic instability with reduced hemoglobin and hematocrit) and signs of ongoing bleeding should receive a transfusion pending confirmation of any explicitly stated preference.</p><p>An informed, competent adult has the right to refuse blood transfusion, even if the blood may be lifesaving. One of the most common reasons for declining blood transfusion is identification as a Jehovah's witness. Many Jehovah's witnesses will not accept transfusions of whole blood, red blood cells, platelets, or plasma, although many will accept albumin. Additionally, they often decline autologous blood transfusions, although they often accept cardiopulmonary bypass. This patient is most likely experiencing traumatic hemorrhagic shock, based on his tachycardia, hypotension, positive focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST) exam, and hematocrit, and appropriate resuscitation should occur with fluid and blood products to restore hemodynamic stability while he undergoes emergent surgical exploration. This patient is unable to consent, as he is only alert and oriented to self, and has no records that identify him as being a Jehovah's witness. While his friend believes he may be a Jehovah's witness, she is unsure, and there is no way to confirm this as his family is unable to be reached. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management of this patient is transfusion of packed red blood cells, as a delay in providing blood products in this hemodynamically unstable patient would likely lead to significant morbidity and mortality.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, and D.</p><p>Immediate consultation with the hospital ethics committee (Choice A) would not be warranted in this patient. A delay in providing care to this patient to consult the ethics committee would lead to worsening of this patient's shock and a significant increase in his mortality. As this patient's status as a Jehovah's witness cannot be confirmed, he should receive a blood transfusion.</p><p>Intravenous administration of fluids only (Choice B) may be initially warranted in hypovolemic shock. However, with significant enough blood loss, hemoglobin and hematocrit decrease, and the patient will continue to be hypotensive despite fluid administration. This patient has hypotension with a hematocrit of 15% and ongoing blood loss, which warrants red blood cell transfusion.</p><p>Withholding treatment until the patient's Jehovah's Witness status can be confirmed (Choice D) is inappropriate. A delay in providing care to this patient would lead to worsening of this patient's shock and a significant increase in his mortality.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients frequently lack the capacity to make medical decisions for a variety of reasons, especially when critically ill. In these scenarios, health care providers must discuss medical decisions with the healthcare proxy, which is often the closest next of kin. If a healthcare proxy is unavailable, a court-appointed legal guardian may be provided to assist with medical decision-making.</p><p>Scenarios frequently arise in healthcare in which patients are unable to consent for themselves. For example, patients with altered mental status or who are intubated and sedated lack the capacity to make decisions, which may be caused by the inability to understand the consent process or the inability to vocalize their desires. In this situation, the healthcare provider must receive consent from the patient's health care proxy. While different states have varying laws about the hierarchy of decision-makers, spouses, parents, and siblings are often included. This patient has had ongoing mechanical ventilatory requirements, and she is unlikely to wean from the mechanical ventilator soon given her recurrent hypoxia and hypercarbia. Prolonged orotracheal intubation can lead to laryngeal edema, tracheal stenosis, and oropharyngeal ulcers, whereas the placement of a tracheostomy improves patient comfort, allows them to communicate, and facilitates the ventilator weaning process. Thus, it would be in this patient's best interest to undergo tracheostomy placement. As her wishes are unknown, the most appropriate next step in management would be to reach out to her next of kin, in this case her sister, to obtain consent for the procedure.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Continuing current ventilator support with orotracheal intubation (Choice A) may be appropriate until her next of kin can be contacted. However, ongoing orotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation can lead to patient discomfort, laryngeal edema, tracheal stenosis, and oropharyngeal ulcers. Transitioning this patient to ventilation through a tracheostomy tube would be in her best interest.</p><p>Contact the hospital ethics committee to give consent (Choice C) is not necessary, as this patient still has next of kin that could be contacted to make decisions for her. If the patient's sister cannot be contacted, then procedures that are deemed in the best interest of the patient can often proceed without consent. Alternatively, a court-appointed legal guardian can be assigned to assist with medical decision-making.</p><p>Discontinue sedation and determine if the patient can communicate her wishes (Choice D) is inappropriate. Attempts to wean this patient's sedation in the past have caused life- threatening hypoxia and hypercarbia. This would likely occur with additional attempts to wean her sedation, so attempting to determine the patient's wishes would put her at an unacceptable level of risk.</p><p>Proceed with tracheostomy as a medical necessity (Choice E) is inappropriate. The patient still has next of kin that could consent to the procedure or that would have a better idea of this patient's goals of care. If the sister cannot be contacted, procedures that are deemed medically necessary and time-sensitive can often proceed without consent. Alternatively, a court-appointed legal guardian can be assigned to assist with medical decision-making.</p>"};
